{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PazgoSe6ULcJzYswiSrWHA","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Cadres Coding Standard\n2\t\n3\tAspirational: do it right the first time. No shortcuts, no MVP, no stubs, no deferrals, no half-finished work. Enterprise grade on day one.\n4\t\n5\tThis document says **how** we write code. Framework-specific landmines (SQLAlchemy `Enum`, Pydantic v2 vs v1, bcrypt 72-byte limit, etc.) live in `/home/pete/cadres/portal/CLAUDE.md`.\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## Prime directive\n10\t\n11\tCode is read 10× more than written. Optimize for the reader.\n12\t\n13\tIf you're tempted to leave a mess and \"clean it up later,\" stop. Later doesn't come. The cost compounds with every reader who has to decode it.\n14\t\n15\t---\n16\t\n17\t## Hard limits\n18\t\n19\tCeilings, not targets. Cross them deliberately, with a one-line justification in the commit message.\n20\t\n21\t| Unit | Max | When exceeded |\n22\t|---|---|---|\n23\t| File | 800 lines | Split along a coherent boundary (tabs, service modules, sub-routers, hooks). |\n24\t| Function | 50 lines | Extract helpers. If you can't name the helpers clearly, the function is doing too many things. |\n25\t| Nesting | 3 levels | Flatten with early returns, guard clauses, or extraction. No pyramid of doom. |\n26\t| Positional args | 5 | Group into a dataclass / TypedDict / Pydantic model. |\n27\t| Cyclomatic complexity | 10 per function | Same answer as above. |\n28\t| `useState` per component | 10 | Group into `useReducer` or custom hooks. |\n29\t| Test length | 30 lines | If setup is larger than the assertion, the abstraction is wrong — factor into fixtures or builder helpers. |\n30\t\n31\tNumbers are heuristics: an 850-line file with 9 coherent sections is fine; a 400-line file doing 6 unrelated things is not. But cross a limit without a clear reason and you're making the codebase worse.\n32\t\n33\t---\n34\t\n35\t## Single source of truth\n36\t\n37\tEvery piece of knowledge lives in exactly one place.\n38\t\n39\t- **Duplicate detection before writing.** Grep for the name, the signature, or a characteristic line of the logic. If it already exists, import it. If it almost exists, extend the existing helper — don't fork.\n40\t- **Copy-paste is a design smell.** When you catch yourself duplicating, stop and extract. If you extract mid-way through a task, migrate every caller in the same commit — partial migration is worse than none.\n41\t- **When three places duplicate, extract.** Two can be coincidence; three is a pattern.\n42\t\n43\t### Canonical helpers for this codebase\n44\t\n45\tUpdate this list when new ones appear. Never invent a parallel helper.\n46\t\n47\t**Backend**\n48\t- `core.rbac.require_permission(...)` — every new endpoint\n49\t- `core.rbac.ensure_operator_tenant(...)` — operator-only tenant guards\n50\t- `core.rbac.PORTAL_PERMISSIONS` / `PERMISSION_DESCRIPTIONS` — canonical permission catalog\n51\t- `core.security.hash_client_secret` / `verify_client_secret` — OIDC / client credential hashing\n52\t- `core.security.hash_password` / `verify_password` — user password hashing (bcrypt, separate from client secrets)\n53\t- `core.encryption._derive_fernet_key` (lru-cached) — Fernet encryption at rest\n54\t- `core.email._render_html` / `_render_text` — all outbound email (Jinja2 autoescape)\n55\t- `core.services.my_apps.list_my_apps` — app tile resolution for any surface\n56\t- `core.federation.keys.derive_signing_certificate_pem` — SAML cert derivation\n57\t- `core.dependencies.is_access_grant_usable` — runtime grant validity check\n58\t- `sqlalchemy.text(...)` — wrap every raw SQL string\n59\t\n60\t**Frontend**\n61\t- `utils/errors.parseApiError(e)` + `toast.error(...)` — every mutation catch\n62\t- `utils/dates.parseUTCDate(s)` — every ISO-string → Date render\n63\t- `utils/accessMode.formatAccessModeLabel(m)` — access-context label display\n64\t- `i18n.t(key, params)` — every user-facing string; inline English literals in JSX are a code-review block. Import `{ t }` from `src/i18n` (or `useTranslation()` from `react-i18next` in components that need re-render on language change). Compose interpolation through ICU placeholders (`{value}`) or plural rules, not string concatenation. See `ui-specification.md` §14 for the full rule set.\n65\t- `components/common/SearchableSelect` — every ID field (never raw `<select>` or `type=\"number\"` for IDs)\n66\t- `components/common/PasswordStrength` — every new-password input\n67\t- `components/common/SortableHeader` + `hooks/useTableSort` — every sortable table\n68\t- `components/common/PageHeader` — every page header\n69\t- `<dialog>` + `<form method=\"dialog\" className=\"modal-backdrop\">` — every modal (never `<div className=\"modal\">` backdrop, never `window.confirm/prompt/alert`)\n70\t- `services/api` (barrel) — every API call; never construct `axios` instances ad hoc\n71\t\n72\t---\n73\t\n74\t## Error handling\n75\t\n76\tEvery failure path either recovers meaningfully or surfaces loudly.\n77\t\n78\t**Banned**\n79\t- Silent `.catch(() => {})` or `.catch(e => console.error(e))` on user-initiated mutations\n80\t- Bare `except:` or catch-all `except Exception:` without a specific reason documented\n81\t- Ignoring a `Result` / `Optional` return without a one-line reason\n82\t- Generic error messages that hide the real cause (\"Something went wrong\")\n83\t- Swallowing validation errors and returning `200 OK` with an empty payload\n84\t\n85\t**Required**\n86\t- Frontend mutations (POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE): `catch (e) { toast.error(parseApiError(e)); }`. Reads may show an empty state, but never silently.\n87\t- Backend: structured `HTTPException` with accurate status codes and detail bodies. Pydantic validators for input shape. SCIM endpoints use the RFC 7644 error envelope.\n88\t- Validate at system boundaries (request/response, external APIs, DB rows parsed from JSON). Don't re-validate inside trusted code.\n89\t- Log with enough context to diagnose without reproducing (request id, user id, tenant id, and safe-to-log inputs).\n90\t\n91\t---\n92\t\n93\t## Naming\n94\t\n95\tClear names remove 80% of the need for comments.\n96\t\n97\t- **Intent over mechanism.** `isAdmin` not `hasPermissionX`. `cancelRotation()` not `resetState()`.\n98\t- **Full words.** Abbreviations allowed only for a small set: `id`, `url`, `db`, `ctx`, `err`, `req`, `res`, `pk`.\n99\t- **Symmetry.** `create_user` pairs with `delete_user`, not `remove_user`. `load_*` pairs with `save_*`, not `persist_*`.\n100\t- **No generic containers.** `data`, `info`, `result`, `obj`, `stuff` — if you can't name it better, you haven't understood it yet.\n101\t- **Booleans read as questions.** `is_active`, `has_membership`, `can_manage`, `should_retry`.\n102\t- **Verbs lead handlers.** `handleRotateSecret`, `handleSubmit`, `handleChange`. Not noun-only (`rotation`, `submit`).\n103\t- **Types are nouns.** `UserProfile`, not `UserProfileManager` (there is no Manager; it's just the profile).\n104\t\n105\t---\n106\t\n107\t## Consistency over cleverness\n108\t\n109\t- **Match the nearest neighbor.** Read an adjacent file before writing. If it has a pattern, follow it. If you think the pattern is wrong, change it globally — don't diverge locally.\n110\t- **One way to do X per codebase.** Two modal patterns is one too many. Two date renderers is one too many. Two pagination controls is one too many.\n111\t- **Boring is a feature.** A clever one-liner that takes 30 seconds to parse is worse than a boring 8-line loop.\n112\t\n113\t---\n114\t\n115\t## No broken windows\n116\t\n117\tThese are the accumulations that turn \"works\" into \"messy\" over time. Ban them at PR time, not \"later.\"\n118\t\n119\t- **No unjustified suppressions.** `# noqa`, `# type: ignore`, `eslint-disable`, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error` — each requires an inline one-line comment explaining *why the rule is wrong here* (not *what the code does*) on the same line or immediately above. A bare suppression is an admission the rule is wrong or the code is; fix the actual problem instead. If the rule is genuinely wrong for the project, change the rule globally in the config — don't diverge per line.\n120\t- **For file-local structural exceptions, use per-file-ignores.** Some patterns are legitimate in one file only — `load_dotenv()` before env-reading imports, alembic path manipulation, `__init__.py` re-exports that must follow a base-class registration. Use `[lint.per-file-ignores]` in `ruff.toml` or ESLint config `files:` overrides with a comment saying *why this file is special*. This is neither a global rule change nor a per-line suppression; it's the right tool for structural ordering constraints. **Not** for individual violations — those still get fixed in code.\n121\t- **No `TODO` / `FIXME` / `XXX` in committed code.** Either fix it in this change or file an issue with a link.\n122\t- **No commented-out code.** Delete it; git remembers.\n123\t- **No dead imports, dead symbols, dead routes, dead feature flags.** Remove as part of the change that made them dead.\n124\t- **No `window.confirm / prompt / alert`.** Use `<dialog>`.\n125\t- **No duplicate logic surviving a refactor.** If you extract a helper, migrate every caller in the same commit.\n126\t- **No leftover scaffolding.** Test fixtures for features that didn't ship, commented placeholder imports, half-written classes — delete them.\n127\t\n128\t---\n129\t\n130\t## Schema / model parity\n131\t\n132\tPublic API schemas mirror persistence models unless intentional.\n133\t\n134\t- Every non-private column on the model has a field in the relevant response schema, or an explicit reason to omit (e.g. `password_hash`).\n135\t- Field types in the schema mirror column nullability: `nullable=False` column → required schema field; `nullable=True` → `Optional`.\n136\t- If a schema field is derived (not backed by a column), document it in the field's docstring with a pointer to the serializer that computes it. Example: `SupportAccessResponse.tenant_access_grant_id` is derived — noted in its docstring.\n137\t- Update schema + migration + test together. Schema drift is a latent bug.\n138\t\n139\t---\n140\t\n141\t## Tests as documentation\n142\t\n143\t- **One test per behavior**, named for the behavior: `test_delete_last_admin_returns_409`, not `test_delete_user_1`.\n144\t- Tests read like a spec. A new reader should learn what the code does from the test list alone.\n145\t- Integration tests prefer real collaborators (real DB, real Jinja renderer, real SAML XML). Mock only at the process boundary (outbound HTTP, SMTP). Don't mock within the domain.\n146\t- Test the public boundary, not the implementation. Refactors shouldn't break tests; behavior changes should.\n147\t- Every bug gets a regression test in the same commit.\n148\t\n149\t---\n150\t\n151\t## Observability\n152\t\n153\tIf it can't be debugged from logs and the audit trail without reproduction, it isn't finished.\n154\t\n155\t- Every user-visible action emits a log line and, where appropriate, an audit record.\n156\t- Every error log includes request/user/tenant context.\n157\t- Every mutation crosses through the audit helper before `db.commit()` so the audit shares the business transaction.\n158\t- Webhook / email / external-service failures log at `warning` or `error` — never silently retry into the void.\n159\t\n160\t---\n161\t\n162\t## Done means done\n163\t\n164\tBefore claiming a task complete, confirm every checkbox:\n165\t\n166\t- [ ] Code change achieves the stated outcome — not \"mostly,\" actually.\n167\t- [ ] Build is clean (`cd frontend && npm run build` / `pytest tests -k <scope>`) with no new warnings you introduced.\n168\t- [ ] Diff contains zero `TODO` / `FIXME` / `eslint-disable` / `@ts-ignore` / silent `console.error` / bare `except:`.\n169\t- [ ] All N duplicates migrated, not just the first one.\n170\t- [ ] Docs updated in the same commit — manual, functional, architecture, todo, plan doc.\n171\t- [ ] Plan file moved to `docs/plans/archive/` if the plan is fully complete.\n172\t- [ ] Observability in place — new failure modes are diagnosable from logs + audit.\n173\t- [ ] Rollback plan exists for destructive or multi-step migrations.\n174\t\n175\t\"It works on my machine\" is a yellow flag, not a green light.\n176\t\n177\t---\n178\t\n179\t## When to decide vs when to ask\n180\t\n181\tReversibility + blast radius determine the answer.\n182\t\n183\t| Action | Default |\n184\t|---|---|\n185\t| Local reversible (edit a file, add a test, run tests) | Decide and proceed |\n186\t| Shared / visible (push, comment on PR, send message) | Confirm first unless pre-authorized for this exact action |\n187\t| Destructive (force-push, drop table, `rm -rf`, `--no-verify`) | Always confirm, even if authorized for a similar action |\n188\t| Infra-shared (deploy, restart prod service, change CI) | Always confirm, even in dev, unless the user named the specific action |\n189\t\n190\tMatch the scope of your action to what was asked. Authorization for one thing is not authorization for the next.\n191\t\n192\t---\n193\t\n194\t## What this isn't\n195\t\n196\tThis is the shape and quality layer. For project-specific framework gotchas (Enum columns, `text()` wrapping, bcrypt 72-byte limit, Pydantic v2 config, cookie + JWT auth, etc.) see each project's own `CLAUDE.md`.\n197\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"363b4a1a-84cd-46bc-a301-3b0ae0010fd3","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:17:45.889Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md","content":"# Cadres Coding Standard\n\nAspirational: do it right the first time. No shortcuts, no MVP, no stubs, no deferrals, no half-finished work. Enterprise grade on day one.\n\nThis document says **how** we write code. Framework-specific landmines (SQLAlchemy `Enum`, Pydantic v2 vs v1, bcrypt 72-byte limit, etc.) live in `/home/pete/cadres/portal/CLAUDE.md`.\n\n---\n\n## Prime directive\n\nCode is read 10× more than written. Optimize for the reader.\n\nIf you're tempted to leave a mess and \"clean it up later,\" stop. Later doesn't come. The cost compounds with every reader who has to decode it.\n\n---\n\n## Hard limits\n\nCeilings, not targets. Cross them deliberately, with a one-line justification in the commit message.\n\n| Unit | Max | When exceeded |\n|---|---|---|\n| File | 800 lines | Split along a coherent boundary (tabs, service modules, sub-routers, hooks). |\n| Function | 50 lines | Extract helpers. If you can't name the helpers clearly, the function is doing too many things. |\n| Nesting | 3 levels | Flatten with early returns, guard clauses, or extraction. No pyramid of doom. |\n| Positional args | 5 | Group into a dataclass / TypedDict / Pydantic model. |\n| Cyclomatic complexity | 10 per function | Same answer as above. |\n| `useState` per component | 10 | Group into `useReducer` or custom hooks. |\n| Test length | 30 lines | If setup is larger than the assertion, the abstraction is wrong — factor into fixtures or builder helpers. |\n\nNumbers are heuristics: an 850-line file with 9 coherent sections is fine; a 400-line file doing 6 unrelated things is not. But cross a limit without a clear reason and you're making the codebase worse.\n\n---\n\n## Single source of truth\n\nEvery piece of knowledge lives in exactly one place.\n\n- **Duplicate detection before writing.** Grep for the name, the signature, or a characteristic line of the logic. If it already exists, import it. If it almost exists, extend the existing helper — don't fork.\n- **Copy-paste is a design smell.** When you catch yourself duplicating, stop and extract. If you extract mid-way through a task, migrate every caller in the same commit — partial migration is worse than none.\n- **When three places duplicate, extract.** Two can be coincidence; three is a pattern.\n\n### Canonical helpers for this codebase\n\nUpdate this list when new ones appear. Never invent a parallel helper.\n\n**Backend**\n- `core.rbac.require_permission(...)` — every new endpoint\n- `core.rbac.ensure_operator_tenant(...)` — operator-only tenant guards\n- `core.rbac.PORTAL_PERMISSIONS` / `PERMISSION_DESCRIPTIONS` — canonical permission catalog\n- `core.security.hash_client_secret` / `verify_client_secret` — OIDC / client credential hashing\n- `core.security.hash_password` / `verify_password` — user password hashing (bcrypt, separate from client secrets)\n- `core.encryption._derive_fernet_key` (lru-cached) — Fernet encryption at rest\n- `core.email._render_html` / `_render_text` — all outbound email (Jinja2 autoescape)\n- `core.services.my_apps.list_my_apps` — app tile resolution for any surface\n- `core.federation.keys.derive_signing_certificate_pem` — SAML cert derivation\n- `core.dependencies.is_access_grant_usable` — runtime grant validity check\n- `sqlalchemy.text(...)` — wrap every raw SQL string\n\n**Frontend**\n- `utils/errors.parseApiError(e)` + `toast.error(...)` — every mutation catch\n- `utils/dates.parseUTCDate(s)` — every ISO-string → Date render\n- `utils/accessMode.formatAccessModeLabel(m)` — access-context label display\n- `i18n.t(key, params)` — every user-facing string; inline English literals in JSX are a code-review block. Import `{ t }` from `src/i18n` (or `useTranslation()` from `react-i18next` in components that need re-render on language change). Compose interpolation through ICU placeholders (`{value}`) or plural rules, not string concatenation. See `ui-specification.md` §14 for the full rule set.\n- `components/common/SearchableSelect` — every ID field (never raw `<select>` or `type=\"number\"` for IDs)\n- `components/common/PasswordStrength` — every new-password input\n- `components/common/SortableHeader` + `hooks/useTableSort` — every sortable table\n- `components/common/PageHeader` — every page header\n- `<dialog>` + `<form method=\"dialog\" className=\"modal-backdrop\">` — every modal (never `<div className=\"modal\">` backdrop, never `window.confirm/prompt/alert`)\n- `services/api` (barrel) — every API call; never construct `axios` instances ad hoc\n\n---\n\n## Error handling\n\nEvery failure path either recovers meaningfully or surfaces loudly.\n\n**Banned**\n- Silent `.catch(() => {})` or `.catch(e => console.error(e))` on user-initiated mutations\n- Bare `except:` or catch-all `except Exception:` without a specific reason documented\n- Ignoring a `Result` / `Optional` return without a one-line reason\n- Generic error messages that hide the real cause (\"Something went wrong\")\n- Swallowing validation errors and returning `200 OK` with an empty payload\n\n**Required**\n- Frontend mutations (POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE): `catch (e) { toast.error(parseApiError(e)); }`. Reads may show an empty state, but never silently.\n- Backend: structured `HTTPException` with accurate status codes and detail bodies. Pydantic validators for input shape. SCIM endpoints use the RFC 7644 error envelope.\n- Validate at system boundaries (request/response, external APIs, DB rows parsed from JSON). Don't re-validate inside trusted code.\n- Log with enough context to diagnose without reproducing (request id, user id, tenant id, and safe-to-log inputs).\n\n---\n\n## Naming\n\nClear names remove 80% of the need for comments.\n\n- **Intent over mechanism.** `isAdmin` not `hasPermissionX`. `cancelRotation()` not `resetState()`.\n- **Full words.** Abbreviations allowed only for a small set: `id`, `url`, `db`, `ctx`, `err`, `req`, `res`, `pk`.\n- **Symmetry.** `create_user` pairs with `delete_user`, not `remove_user`. `load_*` pairs with `save_*`, not `persist_*`.\n- **No generic containers.** `data`, `info`, `result`, `obj`, `stuff` — if you can't name it better, you haven't understood it yet.\n- **Booleans read as questions.** `is_active`, `has_membership`, `can_manage`, `should_retry`.\n- **Verbs lead handlers.** `handleRotateSecret`, `handleSubmit`, `handleChange`. Not noun-only (`rotation`, `submit`).\n- **Types are nouns.** `UserProfile`, not `UserProfileManager` (there is no Manager; it's just the profile).\n\n---\n\n## Consistency over cleverness\n\n- **Match the nearest neighbor.** Read an adjacent file before writing. If it has a pattern, follow it. If you think the pattern is wrong, change it globally — don't diverge locally.\n- **One way to do X per codebase.** Two modal patterns is one too many. Two date renderers is one too many. Two pagination controls is one too many.\n- **Boring is a feature.** A clever one-liner that takes 30 seconds to parse is worse than a boring 8-line loop.\n\n---\n\n## No broken windows\n\nThese are the accumulations that turn \"works\" into \"messy\" over time. Ban them at PR time, not \"later.\"\n\n- **No unjustified suppressions.** `# noqa`, `# type: ignore`, `eslint-disable`, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error` — each requires an inline one-line comment explaining *why the rule is wrong here* (not *what the code does*) on the same line or immediately above. A bare suppression is an admission the rule is wrong or the code is; fix the actual problem instead. If the rule is genuinely wrong for the project, change the rule globally in the config — don't diverge per line.\n- **For file-local structural exceptions, use per-file-ignores.** Some patterns are legitimate in one file only — `load_dotenv()` before env-reading imports, alembic path manipulation, `__init__.py` re-exports that must follow a base-class registration. Use `[lint.per-file-ignores]` in `ruff.toml` or ESLint config `files:` overrides with a comment saying *why this file is special*. This is neither a global rule change nor a per-line suppression; it's the right tool for structural ordering constraints. **Not** for individual violations — those still get fixed in code.\n- **No `TODO` / `FIXME` / `XXX` in committed code.** Either fix it in this change or file an issue with a link.\n- **No commented-out code.** Delete it; git remembers.\n- **No dead imports, dead symbols, dead routes, dead feature flags.** Remove as part of the change that made them dead.\n- **No `window.confirm / prompt / alert`.** Use `<dialog>`.\n- **No duplicate logic surviving a refactor.** If you extract a helper, migrate every caller in the same commit.\n- **No leftover scaffolding.** Test fixtures for features that didn't ship, commented placeholder imports, half-written classes — delete them.\n\n---\n\n## Schema / model parity\n\nPublic API schemas mirror persistence models unless intentional.\n\n- Every non-private column on the model has a field in the relevant response schema, or an explicit reason to omit (e.g. `password_hash`).\n- Field types in the schema mirror column nullability: `nullable=False` column → required schema field; `nullable=True` → `Optional`.\n- If a schema field is derived (not backed by a column), document it in the field's docstring with a pointer to the serializer that computes it. Example: `SupportAccessResponse.tenant_access_grant_id` is derived — noted in its docstring.\n- Update schema + migration + test together. Schema drift is a latent bug.\n\n---\n\n## Tests as documentation\n\n- **One test per behavior**, named for the behavior: `test_delete_last_admin_returns_409`, not `test_delete_user_1`.\n- Tests read like a spec. A new reader should learn what the code does from the test list alone.\n- Integration tests prefer real collaborators (real DB, real Jinja renderer, real SAML XML). Mock only at the process boundary (outbound HTTP, SMTP). Don't mock within the domain.\n- Test the public boundary, not the implementation. Refactors shouldn't break tests; behavior changes should.\n- Every bug gets a regression test in the same commit.\n\n---\n\n## Observability\n\nIf it can't be debugged from logs and the audit trail without reproduction, it isn't finished.\n\n- Every user-visible action emits a log line and, where appropriate, an audit record.\n- Every error log includes request/user/tenant context.\n- Every mutation crosses through the audit helper before `db.commit()` so the audit shares the business transaction.\n- Webhook / email / external-service failures log at `warning` or `error` — never silently retry into the void.\n\n---\n\n## Done means done\n\nBefore claiming a task complete, confirm every checkbox:\n\n- [ ] Code change achieves the stated outcome — not \"mostly,\" actually.\n- [ ] Build is clean (`cd frontend && npm run build` / `pytest tests -k <scope>`) with no new warnings you introduced.\n- [ ] Diff contains zero `TODO` / `FIXME` / `eslint-disable` / `@ts-ignore` / silent `console.error` / bare `except:`.\n- [ ] All N duplicates migrated, not just the first one.\n- [ ] Docs updated in the same commit — manual, functional, architecture, todo, plan doc.\n- [ ] Plan file moved to `docs/plans/archive/` if the plan is fully complete.\n- [ ] Observability in place — new failure modes are diagnosable from logs + audit.\n- [ ] Rollback plan exists for destructive or multi-step migrations.\n\n\"It works on my machine\" is a yellow flag, not a green light.\n\n---\n\n## When to decide vs when to ask\n\nReversibility + blast radius determine the answer.\n\n| Action | Default |\n|---|---|\n| Local reversible (edit a file, add a test, run tests) | Decide and proceed |\n| Shared / visible (push, comment on PR, send message) | Confirm first unless pre-authorized for this exact action |\n| Destructive (force-push, drop table, `rm -rf`, `--no-verify`) | Always confirm, even if authorized for a similar action |\n| Infra-shared (deploy, restart prod service, change CI) | Always confirm, even in dev, unless the user named the specific action |\n\nMatch the scope of your action to what was asked. Authorization for one thing is not authorization for the next.\n\n---\n\n## What this isn't\n\nThis is the shape and quality layer. For project-specific framework gotchas (Enum columns, `text()` wrapping, bcrypt 72-byte limit, Pydantic v2 config, cookie + JWT auth, etc.) see each project's own `CLAUDE.md`.\n","numLines":197,"startLine":1,"totalLines":197}}}
