{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01A8zJ5KS1EYbZ3JsTXEyRxe","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Lattice Project Instructions\n2\t\n3\t## Lattice Context Engine — Available Tools\n4\t\n5\tLattice provides a dependency graph and context engine for this codebase.\n6\tIf you're unsure which tool to use, default to `prepare_change` for implementation tasks and `get_context_capsule` for understanding tasks.\n7\tIf the task starts from a failing test, stack trace, or compiler error, start with `diagnose_failure` and use `prepare_change` after it narrows the likely culprit.\n8\t\n9\tUse these tools when they're the best fit:\n10\t\n11\t- `prepare_change` — first choice for \"fix/add/refactor X\" once you know the area to change and want likely edit files, tests, risks, and nearby memory in one result\n12\t- `get_context_capsule` — first choice for unfamiliar subsystems or broad questions (use `mode: \"focused\"` for targeted lookups)\n13\t- `get_skeleton` — use before opening a large file when you want structure without full source\n14\t- `impact_from_diff` — when reviewing a diff or local change and you want downstream impact plus tests\n15\t- `find_relevant_tests` — when deciding what tests to run for a file, symbol, or diff\n16\t- `diagnose_failure` — first choice when a fix starts from a stack trace, compiler error, or failing test and you need likely culprit symbols before planning the change\n17\t- `expand_context` — when a prior workflow call returned a handle and you only want the next delta, not a full bundle again\n18\t- `get_working_set_context` — only when you already have a few open files and want a compressed working set bundle instead of reading them one by one\n19\t- `get_impact_graph` — before refactoring to understand blast radius\n20\t- `search_symbols` — when looking for a symbol by name across the project\n21\t- `search_logic_flow` — to trace call chains between functions\n22\t- `get_docs_capsule` — first choice when the answer likely lives in Markdown docs, ADRs, runbooks, or scorecards\n23\t- `get_backlinks` / `get_outgoing_links` — inspect how docs, sections, files, and symbols reference each other\n24\t- `find_stale_docs` — identify docs that may be outdated after code or symbol changes\n25\t- `workspace_setup` / `get_project_rules` — refresh detected repo conventions and language setup\n26\t- `save_observation` / `get_session_context` / `search_memory` — persist and recall insights across sessions\n27\t- `list_observations` — to review stored memories and clean up stale ones\n28\t- `list_stale_memories` — to find memories that likely need to be refreshed\n29\t- `promote_observation` / `refresh_memory` — to keep durable memory accurate instead of re-creating it\n30\t- `update_observation` — to edit an existing observation's content in-place\n31\t- `delete_observation` — to remove obsolete or incorrect memories\n32\t- `record_workflow_outcome` — save successful implementation outcomes so later sessions can reuse them\n33\t\n34\tFor targeted edits to known files, Read/Grep/Edit are fine.\n35\tLattice adds the most value when you don't already know where to look.\n36\t\n37\t## Markdown Heading References\n38\t\n39\tUse exact Markdown file + heading references when documented behavior is relevant to the change.\n40\t\n41\t- In final summaries, review comments, and saved observations, cite the exact doc path and heading when architecture, workflow, policy, or operational behavior came from docs.\n42\t- Prefer updating the relevant Markdown section and referencing that heading instead of duplicating the same explanation in code comments.\n43\t- Only add inline code comments that reference a Markdown heading when the behavior is non-obvious and the Markdown section is the authoritative explanation.\n44\t- Do not add doc-heading references to routine comments, obvious code, or trivial implementation details.\n45\t\n46\t\n47\t## Project Structure\n48\t\n49\t- `extension/` — VS Code extension (TypeScript), communicates with daemon over JSON-RPC stdio\n50\t- `daemon/` — Rust backend with tree-sitter parsing, petgraph dependency graph, SQLite storage\n51\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/` — Core library (parser, graph, query engine, indexer, storage)\n52\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/` — Binary entry point + RPC server\n53\t\n54\t## Build\n55\t\n56\t- Daemon: `cd daemon && cargo build --release`\n57\t- Extension: `cd extension && npm install && npm run compile`\n58\t- Tests: `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace`\n59\t\n60\t## Deploy\n61\t\n62\tAfter building, update both binary locations:\n63\t\n64\t```\n65\tpkill -f lattice && sleep 2\n66\tcp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/\n67\tcp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/\n68\t```\n69\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01LgCYkkLu5MSqvwzg2iCDvg","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"ad295ea9-2aed-40ed-8a62-3a4fde22d7e4","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:18:24.539Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Explore docs and plans"}
