{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01N341T8sw67b2w1SfE735Xg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Structured Memory\n2\t\n3\t## Summary\n4\t\n5\tPhase 5 introduces a structured assertion layer on top of the existing durable memory store. The goal is not to replace freeform memory content, but to persist enough verification, provenance, freshness, and relationship metadata that later assistant workflows can reuse memory with an explainable trust order.\n6\t\n7\tThe current implementation is split between:\n8\t\n9\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/model.rs` for the structured-memory types\n10\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/store.rs` for SQLite persistence, additive migration, default derivation, and recall ordering\n11\t- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/agent.rs` for assistant-facing ranking, highlight generation, and compact/full memory bundle shaping\n12\t\n13\tThis note describes only behavior verified in those areas. It does not define a new daemon or MCP contract.\n14\t\n15\t## Why This Exists\n16\t\n17\tBefore Phase 5, memory rows were durable and searchable, but most trust judgment still had to be inferred from freeform text, confidence, and recency. That makes reuse fragile in longer coding sessions:\n18\t\n19\t- a newer note can be weaker than an older verified one\n20\t- an observation can be stale after file or symbol changes\n21\t- an old memory can be contradicted or superseded without assistants seeing that clearly\n22\t- workflow outputs can reuse memory without telling the assistant why a memory should be trusted\n23\t\n24\tThe structured layer makes those states explicit so memory reuse can be both safer and easier to explain.\n25\t\n26\t## Data Model\n27\t\n28\tEach memory row still keeps the original core fields such as `content`, `memory_type`, `scope`, `confidence`, `linked_symbols`, `linked_files`, `workspace_id`, `branch`, `refresh_key`, and stale flags.\n29\t\n30\tPhase 5 adds a parallel structured assertion model:\n31\t\n32\t- `assertion_type`\n33\t- `verification_status`\n34\t- `confidence_reason`\n35\t- `supersedes_memory_id`\n36\t- `superseded_by_memory_id`\n37\t- `contradicts_memory_ids`\n38\t- `contradicted_by_memory_ids`\n39\t- `freshness_policy`\n40\t- `freshness_policy_detail`\n41\t- `provenance`\n42\t- `evidence`\n43\t\n44\tThe assertion vocabulary is wider than the legacy `memory_type`. In addition to observation, decision, exploration, pattern, and anti-pattern, the structured layer also supports `workflow_outcome` and `constraint`.\n45\t\n46\tThe verification lifecycle currently supports:\n47\t\n48\t- `unverified`\n49\t- `in_review`\n50\t- `verified`\n51\t- `stale`\n52\t- `contradicted`\n53\t- `superseded`\n54\t\n55\tFreshness is also modeled explicitly through `session_scoped`, `branch_scoped`, `repo_scoped`, `time_bound`, and `manual_review`.\n56\t\n57\t## Persistence And Migration\n58\t\n59\tStructured memory is stored in the existing `memories` SQLite table. The schema is extended additively with new scalar columns and JSON columns for contradiction edges, provenance, and evidence.\n60\t\n61\tThe migration approach is intentionally low-risk:\n62\t\n63\t- `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` defines the full current schema for fresh databases\n64\t- older databases are upgraded with additive `ALTER TABLE` statements for each structured column\n65\t- new indexes are added for `verification_status` and `superseded_by_memory_id`\n66\t- the FTS table is rebuilt after initialization so legacy rows remain searchable\n67\t\n68\tThis means old databases continue to open without a destructive rewrite. Legacy rows receive column defaults at the database level. Structured defaults are then derived on later store/update paths when Lattice rewrites a memory row.\n69\t\n70\t## Default Structured Derivation\n71\t\n72\t`MemoryStore::store` resolves structured metadata before persisting a row.\n73\t\n74\tFor a row that already has structured fields, the store preserves them and reconciles a few fields with current row state:\n75\t\n76\t- extended `assertion_type` values such as `workflow_outcome` and `constraint` are preserved instead of being reset from the legacy `memory_type`\n77\t- legacy-compatible assertion types continue to follow `memory_type`\n78\t- scope-derived freshness policies are recomputed from the current scope\n79\t- `stale` is forced if `memory.is_stale` is true\n80\t- a previously stale row falls back to inferred verification when it is no longer stale"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01BbdQyD8DbWiNRxRFHe822D","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"0c316e43-3e69-4ba6-9d3a-edeb71a0f6e3","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:18:30.397Z","subagent_type":"Explore","task_description":"Explore extension + MCP surface"}
