{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LgCYkkLu5MSqvwzg2iCDvg","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have enough information. Let me compile the report:\n\n## Lattice Documentation Audit Report\n\n### 1. Architecture Files (docs/architecture/)\n\nAll five files are recent (April 2026) design/implementation notes:\n\n- **2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md** — Workflow for `plan_edit`: patch-oriented edit planning that sits on top of change-planning, returning first-edit anchors, caller impacts, stale-doc signals, and candidate spans before full code review.\n- **2026-04-11-structured-memory.md** — Phase 5 memory model: adds structured assertion metadata (verification status, freshness policy, provenance, evidence, contradiction/supersession links) on top of durable store; enables trustworthy memory reuse with explainable ranking.\n- **2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md** — Phase 1 identity system: stable handles (`SymbolId` derived from file, name, byte_offset) for follow-up expansion, replacing ambiguous name-only targets; backward-compatible with legacy forms.\n- **2026-04-11-scenario-tracing.md** — `trace_scenario` workflow: behavior-focused debugging that returns execution paths, guards, side effects, and failure branches ranked by likelihood without exhaustive coverage claims.\n- **2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md** — Vector indexing redesign: USearch ANN backend (`.lattice/vectors.usearch`) alongside SQLite, FTS5 for memory keyword search, scope-aware retrieval (symbol vs. file-summary vectors), incremental sync on graph changes.\n\n### 2. Plans (docs/plans/) — Current & Archive\n\n**Current fork plan (largest, 968 lines):**\n- **2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md** — Successor system design for major fork: unifies workspace graph (files, symbols, docs, tests, configs), append-only event log, typed memory graph (observation, decision, constraint, pattern, anti-pattern, workflow outcome, failure pattern, procedure, preference, architecture invariant, docs contract, counter-memory), consolidation engine, retrieval engine V1, verification engine. 11-phase roadmap with identity model, event log substrate, memory graph storage, retrieval, working memory, consolidation, verification, workflow engine V2, metrics/evaluation, human review UI, and hardening. Includes measurable success criteria (30% fewer discovery calls, 40% fewer irrelevant reads, 80% memory precision, zero stale/contradicted memory in trusted surfaces).\n\n**Archive/foundational plans:**\n- **2026-04-11-assistant-usefulness-roadmap.md** (174 lines) — Executed Phases 1-5 roadmap focusing on stable handles, scenario tracing, patch-oriented planning, structured memory, and storage backends.\n- **2026-03-13-agent-workflow-roadmap.md** (215 lines) — In-progress roadmap shifting from retrieval to workflow engine; targets reduction in tool round-trips, repeated context, and durable memory improvements.\n- **2026-03-30-unified-knowledge-graph-design.md** (532 lines) — Proposed expansion of code graph to unify docs, Markdown, ADRs, runbooks, memory, and freshness across one local knowledge graph.\n- **2026-02-25-lattice-design.md** (484 lines) — Original approved design doc: problem statement (token waste on irrelevant code), solution (local tree-sitter graph, MCP server, SQLite + vectors, no external APIs).\n- **2026-02-25-lattice-implementation.md** (6,447 lines) — Detailed implementation plan with symbol indexing, graph traversal, embedding pipeline, context caching, query engine, MCP contract, extension UI, tests, and deployment.\n\n### 3. Operator Guides / Runbooks / Migration Guides\n\nNone exist yet. The codebase includes:\n- **Build/deploy snippet in CLAUDE.md** (lines 54-68) — Daemon build, extension npm, test, and deploy steps.\n- **Benchmark test files** — `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/query/benchmark_tests.rs` and `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/benchmark_tests.rs` (test code, not standalone guides).\n\n### 4. Benchmark / Evaluation Docs\n\nNo standalone evaluation guides. The fork plan (Section 2.2.9) defines benchmark requirements:\n- retrieval relevance, token efficiency, stale-memory surfacing, test-pass-after-plan, repeated-failure reduction metrics\n- 30% fewer discovery calls, 40% fewer irrelevant reads, 80% memory precision targets\n- Golden benchmark tasks not yet documented separately.\n\n### 5. CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md Highlights\n\n**CLAUDE.md (69 lines):**\n- Durable instruction surface for Lattice repo behavior\n- Core rules: production-grade first pass, correctness over convenience, workspace safety, error handling as core design, verification before completion\n- Communication style: direct, factual, critical by default; no empty praise\n- Markdown heading references: cite exact doc paths when architecture/behavior sourced from docs\n- Build/deploy steps, project structure (daemon/extension split), testing discipline\n\n**AGENTS.md (10,934 bytes, detailed):**\n- Codex-specific instruction surface for repo-wide behavior\n- Lattice workflow tools priority: `prepare_change` for implementation, `get_context_capsule` for understanding, `diagnose_failure` for failures/traces\n- Backward-compatibility and contract discipline: no silent behavior changes; if MCP shape/workflow metadata must change, update docs and migrations together\n- Documentation rules: update README on public behavior change, update CLAUDE.md on Codex rules change, add durable design notes under `docs/` for architecture/memory/ranking/workflow changes\n- Error handling, definition of done, workspace safety, observability, performance/scale, and project structure sections\n- Explicit prohibition on self-attribution to commits, verification before claiming coverage, and test discipline requirements\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n**Documented:** Architecture (5 files on Phases 1-5 and Phase 4 storage redesign), design lineage (5 plan files from Feb-May 2026), project instructions (CLAUDE.md for Lattice, AGENTS.md for Codex).\n\n**Not Yet Documented:** Operator guides, migration pathways, standalone evaluation/benchmark suite, runbooks, upgrade procedures.\n\n**For the fork:** The cognitive workspace plan is comprehensive but requires creating migration docs (from current Lattice), operator guides (memory review surface, event log inspection, consolidation monitoring), and a benchmark suite with golden tasks. None of those docs exist yet."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"2962a26e-f7ea-4d38-93af-b7e898b6a06a","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:18:57.338Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Explore the Lattice docs at /home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/. Report:\n\n1. All files under docs/architecture/ with a one-line description of each.\n2. All files under docs/plans/ (current and archive) — relevant prior plans for memory, retrieval, event log, consolidation, verification, identity, fork.\n3. Any operator guides, runbooks, or migration guides.\n4. Any benchmark or evaluation docs.\n5. Existing CLAUDE.md and AGENTS.md content highlights (what conventions and rules they prescribe).\n\nThis will inform a new build plan for a major fork. The goal is to understand what's already documented and what new docs need creating. Report in under 600 words.","agentId":"adb617741e2078de2","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have enough information. Let me compile the report:\n\n## Lattice Documentation Audit Report\n\n### 1. Architecture Files (docs/architecture/)\n\nAll five files are recent (April 2026) design/implementation notes:\n\n- **2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md** — Workflow for `plan_edit`: patch-oriented edit planning that sits on top of change-planning, returning first-edit anchors, caller impacts, stale-doc signals, and candidate spans before full code review.\n- **2026-04-11-structured-memory.md** — Phase 5 memory model: ad