{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01EiaC7d272TTvsZ28KyrGdk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_016tifUatafaD2Ng6Jcjw9vg","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/context.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":20211,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":20211,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016juPk15H1M5x3FpzubRVsP","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"13c3ceba-de9e-4069-a910-49046d3ebf26","request_id":"req_011Cb7Mb8aTAigW877oBo3ns","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 0+1 task briefs"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016tifUatafaD2Ng6Jcjw9vg","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Build Context — Cognitive Workspace Fork\n2\t\n3\t**Spec:** [`../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md`](../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md)\n4\t**Build directory:** `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/`\n5\t**Date assembled:** 2026-05-16\n6\t\n7\tThis file briefs the build harness on what the existing Lattice platform already provides, what the spec demands that it does not yet provide, and the engineering invariants every task must honor.\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t## 1. What the spec actually demands\n12\t\n13\tThe spec describes a **workspace cognition substrate** with three first-class sources of truth — **workspace graph**, **event log**, **memory graph** — composed under one daemon with explicit identity, retrieval, working memory, consolidation, verification, MCP surface, and human-review layers.\n14\t\n15\tThe implementation plan in the spec is structured as **eleven sequential phases** (Phase 0 → Phase 11). The implementation slice ordering (event log → memory graph → retrieval → consolidation → verification) is binding: identity must precede the event log, the event log must precede consolidation, and verification must precede retrieval taking memory as trusted. The build harness honors that sequence through `depends_on` edges in `tasks.json`.\n16\t\n17\t**Non-negotiables called out by the spec (Section \"Non-Negotiable Product Properties\"):**\n18\t\n19\t- Local-first by default; no silent broad workspace reads bypassing ignore rules.\n20\t- Deterministic identities for every file, symbol, doc section, event, and memory.\n21\t- Every durable memory has evidence; every retrieved memory has an inclusion reason.\n22\t- Every stale or contradicted memory is surfaced — never hidden behind recency.\n23\t- Every workflow bundle is compact by default; expansion is deliberate via stable handles.\n24\t- Every consolidation pass is recoverable, replayable, observable, and reversible.\n25\t- No unbounded payload growth, graph traversal, or event-log scan on hot paths.\n26\t- Hot-path budgets: identity resolution ≤2ms P99; event write ≤5ms P99 on `prepare_change` / `get_context_capsule` (spec §Phase 1, §Phase 2 DoD).\n27\t- LLM-driven consolidation jobs must produce proposals (never silent writes), record model + prompt hash + response hash, run only in background or manual-review modes, and operate under bounded queue depth with documented cost budgets (spec §Phase 6, \"LLM-driven consolidation\").\n28\t- Event log compaction snapshots are daemon-managed background operations, not manual; snapshot format is versioned and independently readable (spec §Storage Design).\n29\t\n30\t---\n31\t\n32\t## 2. Carry-forward inventory (what Lattice already gives us)\n33\t\n34\t### Daemon (Rust)\n35\t\n36\t- **Crates:** `daemon/crates/lattice-core/`, `daemon/crates/lattice-daemon/`.\n37\t- **Graph:** `petgraph::DiGraph<GraphNode, EdgeKind>` in `crates/lattice-core/src/graph/`; nine edge kinds today (`Calls`, `Imports`, `Implements`, `Extends`, `TypeRef`, `Contains`, `LinksTo`, `Mentions`, `CoChanges`). Will be extended to the families listed in spec §Workspace Graph.\n38\t- **Parsers:** tree-sitter for TypeScript, Python, Rust, Go, Java, Markdown under `crates/lattice-core/src/parser/`.\n39\t- **Indexer:** incremental file → graph indexer with cached `ParsedFile` map and one-shot graph rebuild.\n40\t- **Storage:** SQLite under `.lattice/` with `graph.db` (nodes, edges, file_index, parsed_files) and `memories.db` (rich 80+ column `memories` table with `memories_fts`).\n41\t- **Embeddings:** ONNX runtime, 384-dim L2-normalized vectors loaded from `.lattice/models/`; vector store backed by USearch at `.lattice/vector_index/`.\n42\t- **Intelligence:** workflow tools `prepare_change`, `plan_edit`, `get_context_capsule`, `expand_context`, `get_working_set_context`, `trace_scenario`, `summarize_subsystem`, `impact_from_diff`, `diagnose_failure`, `record_workflow_outcome`.\n43\t- **Structured memory:** verification status (`unverified` → `in_review` → `verified` / `stale` / `contradicted` / `superseded`), freshness policy (`session_scoped` | `branch_scoped` | `repo_scoped`), assertion types, provenance/evidence JSON blobs — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`.\n44\t- **Stable handles:** `SymbolId { file, name, byte_offset }` already used by `expand_context` — documented in `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`.\n45\t\n46\t### MCP / RPC\n47\t\n48\t- JSON-RPC 2.0 over stdio (`crates/lattice-daemon/src/rpc/server.rs`).\n49\t- 43 registered tools dispatched through `McpHandler::handle_tools_call()` (`rpc/mcp.rs`).\n50\t- Context-handle cache (`rpc/context_cache.rs`) and per-session telemetry (`rpc/session_metrics.rs`).\n51\t\n52\t### Extension (TypeScript)\n53\t\n54\t- `extension/src/`: `extension.ts` (activation + 14 commands), `daemon.ts` (RPC client), `sidebar.ts` (webview with delivery-mix + handle-reuse meters), `docsWorkbench.ts` (docs graph), `codelens.ts`, `hover.ts`, `statusbar.ts`.\n55\t- Compile via `cd extension && npm run compile`; lint via `npm run lint`; tests via `npm test` (`node ./out/test/runTest.js`).\n56\t- Activity bar contribution (`viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice`) is the slot the new review UI will extend.\n57\t\n58\t### Tests\n59\t\n60\t- `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace` runs all crates.\n61\t- In-tree `tests.rs` next to each module is the convention (e.g. `storage/tests.rs`, `memory/tests.rs`, `intelligence/agent_tests.rs`).\n62\t- Benchmark fixtures live under `crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/benchmark_tests.rs` and `crates/lattice-core/src/query/benchmark_tests.rs`.\n63\t\n64\t### Architecture docs already present\n65\t\n66\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`\n67\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`\n68\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-patch-oriented-planning.md`\n69\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-scenario-tracing.md`\n70\t- `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md`\n71\t\n72\tReference these from new design notes; do not duplicate their content.\n73\t\n74\t---\n75\t\n76\t## 3. Gap inventory (what the spec needs that does not yet exist)\n77\t\n78\t| Spec section | Status in current codebase | Build action |\n79\t|---|---|---|\n80\t| **Phase 0** — fork-or-extend decision + baseline benchmarks | Not done | Decision doc, baseline benchmark capture |\n81\t| **Phase 1** — unified identity for files, docs, sections, events, memories, handles | Partial: only `SymbolId` is stable today | Generalize identity, add resolver, ambiguity diagnostics |\n82\t| **Phase 2** — append-only event log + 20 event kinds + compaction | Absent | New `events` + `event_payloads` tables, writer/reader API, MCP capture, compaction snapshots |\n83\t| **Phase 3** — memory graph with first-class links, evidence, accesses, scores; `CounterMemory` class | Memory is single-table today | New `memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`; class taxonomy expansion; migration from existing rows |\n84\t| **Phase 4** — Retrieval V1 with intent classifier, anchor resolver, hybrid candidates, explainable scoring | Heuristic intent + keyword/semantic in `query/engine.rs`; no diagnostic reasons | New retrieval pipeline returning inclusion reasons + expansion handles |\n85\t| **Phase 5** — explicit working memory + checkpoints | Implicit prompt assembly today | New `working_memory_checkpoints` table + operations + MCP surface |\n86\t| **Phase 6** — consolidation engine (deterministic + LLM-driven proposals, manual queue, replay-safe) | Absent | New `consolidation_jobs` table, proposal-only writes, LLM provenance |\n87\t| **Phase 7** — verification engine (existence, span, scope, expiry, incremental) | `is_stale` flag exists; no incremental verifier | New verifier + `verification_jobs`; scope-leak prevention; stale-label discipline |\n88\t| **Phase 8** — Workflow Engine V2 + 10 new memory tools (audited for surface size) | Old tools exist; new memory tools absent | Redesign existing tools + add audited memory toolset |\n89\t| **Phase 9** — metrics + golden benchmark suite | Session metrics exist; no golden tasks | New benchmark fixture repo + CLI report |\n90\t| **Phase 10** — Human Review UI (memory inbox, queues, event/retrieval views) | Absent (sidebar shows stats only) | New webviews + JSON-RPC plumbing |\n91\t| **Phase 11** — hardening + full documentation suite | Partial | Large-repo perf, concurrency, recovery, corruption, migration tests; complete doc suite |\n92\t\n93\t---\n94\t\n95\t## 4. Fork-or-extend decision (spec §Phase 0 gate)\n96\t\n97\tThe spec's gate conditions for forking are: breaking `memories` table changes incompatible with migration, identity primary-key changes invalidating indexes, event log requiring incompatible SQLite/WAL changes, or two-or-more of the above.\n98\t\n99\tCurrent evidence suggests **extend on a long-lived branch** is feasible:\n100\t\n101\t- The existing `memories` schema already accommodates most of the new fields; new tables (`memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`) can be added alongside.\n102\t- `SymbolId` already gives stable identity; generalizing to other node families is additive.\n103\t- The event log can be a new SQLite file (`.lattice/events.db`) without disturbing `graph.db` or `memories.db`.\n104\t\n105\t**The fork-or-extend decision is itself the first task (T01).** It is binding for every subsequent task: `depends_on` chains assume the decision documents which repo layout, crate boundaries, and migration policy all subsequent work targets. The harness does not assume the answer.\n106\t\n107\t---\n108\t\n109\t## 5. Shared standards every task must satisfy\n110\t\n111\t| Standard | Location | Where it applies |\n112\t|---|---|---|\n113\t| Cadres coding standard | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` | All code tasks: hard limits (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10), single source of truth, error handling, naming, no broken windows, schema parity, tests as documentation, observability. |\n114\t| UI specification | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md` | All frontend tasks (Phase 10 review UI): every interactive surface follows the spec; no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`, modals via `<dialog>`, i18n via `i18n.t(...)`. |\n115\t| Definition of done | `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md` | The final readiness review task asserts every applicable checklist item is satisfied or explicitly waived. |\n116\t| Lattice project rules | `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/AGENTS.md` | Build/deploy procedure (`pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/`), Markdown heading references for doc-anchored behavior, backward-compatibility discipline. |\n117\t| Execution philosophy | `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` \"Execution Philosophy\" | No deferrals, no workarounds, no half-finished work, enterprise-grade on day one. The fork is implemented end-to-end across all eleven phases. |\n118\t\n119\t---\n120\t\n121\t## 6. Verification discipline\n122\t\n123\tEvery task in `tasks.json` carries `verification_commands` that are real shell commands run from the repo root. Conventions:\n124\t\n125\t- **Doc tasks** verify file existence and presence of required headings via `test -f` + `grep -q \"## Heading\"` chains.\n126\t- **Backend tasks** verify with `cd daemon && cargo build --release` and `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace -- <scope>`. New crate or module tests are pinned to the precise test path (`cargo test -p lattice-core --lib events::tests`) so failures map to a single task.\n127\t- **Schema/migration tasks** include a `cargo test` invocation that exercises the migration on a temp DB, plus a `sqlite3` schema-dump diff against a checked-in golden schema where the spec demands it.\n128\t- **Frontend tasks** verify with `cd extension && npm run compile` and `npm run lint`. Smoke tasks add `npm test`.\n129\t- **Cross-layer contract gates** include MCP schema tests (`cargo test --workspace -- mcp_schema`) and extension/daemon round-trip tests that exercise the new tool surface end-to-end.\n130\t- **Benchmark tasks** include `cargo bench` or a CLI invocation that emits a metrics report file the verifier can `test -f`.\n131\t- **Review tasks** verify by file existence of a structured findings report under `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/reviews/RNN.md` with the required sections.\n132\t\n133\tIf a task's verification cannot be expressed as a real command, the task is split or rewritten until it can.\n134\t\n135\t---\n136\t\n137\t## 7. Sequencing rationale\n138\t\n139\tThe eleven spec phases collapse into the following review groupings:\n140\t\n141\t1. **Foundation review** after Phase 0 — fork/extend decision, baseline benchmarks, crate boundary plan, architecture overview.\n142\t2. **Backend reviews** after Phases 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8 — one per substrate layer.\n143\t3. **Contract gates** at three boundaries: (a) Identity + Event Log + Memory cross-layer, (b) Memory + Verification + Retrieval cross-layer, (c) MCP tool surface schema regression, (d) Extension ↔ Daemon review-UI plumbing.\n144\t4. **Metrics review** after Phase 9.\n145\t5. **Frontend review** after Phase 10.\n146\t6. **Tests + Docs review** after Phase 11.\n147\t7. **Definition-of-Done gate** asserting every shared standard is satisfied.\n148\t8. **Final readiness review** asserting the system is production-grade for repeated agent use.\n149\t\n150\tNo phase is allowed to defer scope from a later phase. No phase ships a workaround when the real implementation is reachable in the same build.\n151\t\n152\t---\n153\t\n154\t## 8. Out of scope (explicitly named so it is not silently dropped)\n155\t\n156\tThe spec lists no items as out of scope. Therefore every section under spec §Implementation Plan, §Storage Design, §MCP Tool Contract Principles, §Documentation Requirements, §Testing Requirements, §Measurable Success Criteria, and §Risks is in scope for this build. The first-implementation-slice ordering is a *priority hint*, not a scope reduction.\n157\t\n158\tThe plan honors that: phases 0 through 11 are each materialized as tasks. The \"First Implementation Slice\" steps map to the earliest tasks in their respective phases — they are sequenced first inside their phase, but every phase ships in full before the build completes.\n159\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016juPk15H1M5x3FpzubRVsP","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"2cad1d0a-0805-46f0-9b60-4b06288290e9","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:31:13.095Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 0+1 task briefs"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01EiaC7d272TTvsZ28KyrGdk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SF9y1JuvhiDPGV4UY61ZzY","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":20211,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":20211,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016juPk15H1M5x3FpzubRVsP","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"508ba7b2-85d0-4585-9853-79b5bec0e127","request_id":"req_011Cb7Mb8aTAigW877oBo3ns","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 0+1 task briefs"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SF9y1JuvhiDPGV4UY61ZzY","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Cadres Coding Standard\n2\t\n3\tAspirational: do it right the first time. No shortcuts, no MVP, no stubs, no deferrals, no half-finished work. Enterprise grade on day one.\n4\t\n5\tThis document says **how** we write code. Framework-specific landmines (SQLAlchemy `Enum`, Pydantic v2 vs v1, bcrypt 72-byte limit, etc.) live in `/home/pete/cadres/portal/CLAUDE.md`.\n6\t\n7\t---\n8\t\n9\t## Prime directive\n10\t\n11\tCode is read 10× more than written. Optimize for the reader.\n12\t\n13\tIf you're tempted to leave a mess and \"clean it up later,\" stop. Later doesn't come. The cost compounds with every reader who has to decode it.\n14\t\n15\t---\n16\t\n17\t## Hard limits\n18\t\n19\tCeilings, not targets. Cross them deliberately, with a one-line justification in the commit message.\n20\t\n21\t| Unit | Max | When exceeded |\n22\t|---|---|---|\n23\t| File | 800 lines | Split along a coherent boundary (tabs, service modules, sub-routers, hooks). |\n24\t| Function | 50 lines | Extract helpers. If you can't name the helpers clearly, the function is doing too many things. |\n25\t| Nesting | 3 levels | Flatten with early returns, guard clauses, or extraction. No pyramid of doom. |\n26\t| Positional args | 5 | Group into a dataclass / TypedDict / Pydantic model. |\n27\t| Cyclomatic complexity | 10 per function | Same answer as above. |\n28\t| `useState` per component | 10 | Group into `useReducer` or custom hooks. |\n29\t| Test length | 30 lines | If setup is larger than the assertion, the abstraction is wrong — factor into fixtures or builder helpers. |\n30\t\n31\tNumbers are heuristics: an 850-line file with 9 coherent sections is fine; a 400-line file doing 6 unrelated things is not. But cross a limit without a clear reason and you're making the codebase worse.\n32\t\n33\t---\n34\t\n35\t## Single source of truth\n36\t\n37\tEvery piece of knowledge lives in exactly one place.\n38\t\n39\t- **Duplicate detection before writing.** Grep for the name, the signature, or a characteristic line of the logic. If it already exists, import it. If it almost exists, extend the existing helper — don't fork.\n40\t- **Copy-paste is a design smell.** When you catch yourself duplicating, stop and extract. If you extract mid-way through a task, migrate every caller in the same commit — partial migration is worse than none.\n41\t- **When three places duplicate, extract.** Two can be coincidence; three is a pattern.\n42\t\n43\t### Canonical helpers for this codebase\n44\t\n45\tUpdate this list when new ones appear. Never invent a parallel helper.\n46\t\n47\t**Backend**\n48\t- `core.rbac.require_permission(...)` — every new endpoint\n49\t- `core.rbac.ensure_operator_tenant(...)` — operator-only tenant guards\n50\t- `core.rbac.PORTAL_PERMISSIONS` / `PERMISSION_DESCRIPTIONS` — canonical permission catalog\n51\t- `core.security.hash_client_secret` / `verify_client_secret` — OIDC / client credential hashing\n52\t- `core.security.hash_password` / `verify_password` — user password hashing (bcrypt, separate from client secrets)\n53\t- `core.encryption._derive_fernet_key` (lru-cached) — Fernet encryption at rest\n54\t- `core.email._render_html` / `_render_text` — all outbound email (Jinja2 autoescape)\n55\t- `core.services.my_apps.list_my_apps` — app tile resolution for any surface\n56\t- `core.federation.keys.derive_signing_certificate_pem` — SAML cert derivation\n57\t- `core.dependencies.is_access_grant_usable` — runtime grant validity check\n58\t- `sqlalchemy.text(...)` — wrap every raw SQL string\n59\t\n60\t**Frontend**\n61\t- `utils/errors.parseApiError(e)` + `toast.error(...)` — every mutation catch\n62\t- `utils/dates.parseUTCDate(s)` — every ISO-string → Date render\n63\t- `utils/accessMode.formatAccessModeLabel(m)` — access-context label display\n64\t- `i18n.t(key, params)` — every user-facing string; inline English literals in JSX are a code-review block. Import `{ t }` from `src/i18n` (or `useTranslation()` from `react-i18next` in components that need re-render on language change). Compose interpolation through ICU placeholders (`{value}`) or plural rules, not string concatenation. See `ui-specification.md` §14 for the full rule set.\n65\t- `components/common/SearchableSelect` — every ID field (never raw `<select>` or `type=\"number\"` for IDs)\n66\t- `components/common/PasswordStrength` — every new-password input\n67\t- `components/common/SortableHeader` + `hooks/useTableSort` — every sortable table\n68\t- `components/common/PageHeader` — every page header\n69\t- `<dialog>` + `<form method=\"dialog\" className=\"modal-backdrop\">` — every modal (never `<div className=\"modal\">` backdrop, never `window.confirm/prompt/alert`)\n70\t- `services/api` (barrel) — every API call; never construct `axios` instances ad hoc\n71\t\n72\t---\n73\t\n74\t## Error handling\n75\t\n76\tEvery failure path either recovers meaningfully or surfaces loudly.\n77\t\n78\t**Banned**\n79\t- Silent `.catch(() => {})` or `.catch(e => console.error(e))` on user-initiated mutations\n80\t- Bare `except:` or catch-all `except Exception:` without a specific reason documented\n81\t- Ignoring a `Result` / `Optional` return without a one-line reason\n82\t- Generic error messages that hide the real cause (\"Something went wrong\")\n83\t- Swallowing validation errors and returning `200 OK` with an empty payload\n84\t\n85\t**Required**\n86\t- Frontend mutations (POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE): `catch (e) { toast.error(parseApiError(e)); }`. Reads may show an empty state, but never silently.\n87\t- Backend: structured `HTTPException` with accurate status codes and detail bodies. Pydantic validators for input shape. SCIM endpoints use the RFC 7644 error envelope.\n88\t- Validate at system boundaries (request/response, external APIs, DB rows parsed from JSON). Don't re-validate inside trusted code.\n89\t- Log with enough context to diagnose without reproducing (request id, user id, tenant id, and safe-to-log inputs).\n90\t\n91\t---\n92\t\n93\t## Naming\n94\t\n95\tClear names remove 80% of the need for comments.\n96\t\n97\t- **Intent over mechanism.** `isAdmin` not `hasPermissionX`. `cancelRotation()` not `resetState()`.\n98\t- **Full words.** Abbreviations allowed only for a small set: `id`, `url`, `db`, `ctx`, `err`, `req`, `res`, `pk`.\n99\t- **Symmetry.** `create_user` pairs with `delete_user`, not `remove_user`. `load_*` pairs with `save_*`, not `persist_*`.\n100\t- **No generic containers.** `data`, `info`, `result`, `obj`, `stuff` — if you can't name it better, you haven't understood it yet.\n101\t- **Booleans read as questions.** `is_active`, `has_membership`, `can_manage`, `should_retry`.\n102\t- **Verbs lead handlers.** `handleRotateSecret`, `handleSubmit`, `handleChange`. Not noun-only (`rotation`, `submit`).\n103\t- **Types are nouns.** `UserProfile`, not `UserProfileManager` (there is no Manager; it's just the profile).\n104\t\n105\t---\n106\t\n107\t## Consistency over cleverness\n108\t\n109\t- **Match the nearest neighbor.** Read an adjacent file before writing. If it has a pattern, follow it. If you think the pattern is wrong, change it globally — don't diverge locally.\n110\t- **One way to do X per codebase.** Two modal patterns is one too many. Two date renderers is one too many. Two pagination controls is one too many.\n111\t- **Boring is a feature.** A clever one-liner that takes 30 seconds to parse is worse than a boring 8-line loop.\n112\t\n113\t---\n114\t\n115\t## No broken windows\n116\t\n117\tThese are the accumulations that turn \"works\" into \"messy\" over time. Ban them at PR time, not \"later.\"\n118\t\n119\t- **No unjustified suppressions.** `# noqa`, `# type: ignore`, `eslint-disable`, `@ts-ignore`, `@ts-expect-error` — each requires an inline one-line comment explaining *why the rule is wrong here* (not *what the code does*) on the same line or immediately above. A bare suppression is an admission the rule is wrong or the code is; fix the actual problem instead. If the rule is genuinely wrong for the project, change the rule globally in the config — don't diverge per line.\n120\t- **For file-local structural exceptions, use per-file-ignores.** Some patterns are legitimate in one file only — `load_dotenv()` before env-reading imports, alembic path manipulation, `__init__.py` re-exports that must follow a base-class registration. Use `[lint.per-file-ignores]` in `ruff.toml` or ESLint config `files:` overrides with a comment saying *why this file is special*. This is neither a global rule change nor a per-line suppression; it's the right tool for structural ordering constraints. **Not** for individual violations — those still get fixed in code.\n121\t- **No `TODO` / `FIXME` / `XXX` in committed code.** Either fix it in this change or file an issue with a link.\n122\t- **No commented-out code.** Delete it; git remembers.\n123\t- **No dead imports, dead symbols, dead routes, dead feature flags.** Remove as part of the change that made them dead.\n124\t- **No `window.confirm / prompt / alert`.** Use `<dialog>`.\n125\t- **No duplicate logic surviving a refactor.** If you extract a helper, migrate every caller in the same commit.\n126\t- **No leftover scaffolding.** Test fixtures for features that didn't ship, commented placeholder imports, half-written classes — delete them.\n127\t\n128\t---\n129\t\n130\t## Schema / model parity\n131\t\n132\tPublic API schemas mirror persistence models unless intentional.\n133\t\n134\t- Every non-private column on the model has a field in the relevant response schema, or an explicit reason to omit (e.g. `password_hash`).\n135\t- Field types in the schema mirror column nullability: `nullable=False` column → required schema field; `nullable=True` → `Optional`.\n136\t- If a schema field is derived (not backed by a column), document it in the field's docstring with a pointer to the serializer that computes it. Example: `SupportAccessResponse.tenant_access_grant_id` is derived — noted in its docstring.\n137\t- Update schema + migration + test together. Schema drift is a latent bug.\n138\t\n139\t---\n140\t\n141\t## Tests as documentation\n142\t\n143\t- **One test per behavior**, named for the behavior: `test_delete_last_admin_returns_409`, not `test_delete_user_1`.\n144\t- Tests read like a spec. A new reader should learn what the code does from the test list alone.\n145\t- Integration tests prefer real collaborators (real DB, real Jinja renderer, real SAML XML). Mock only at the process boundary (outbound HTTP, SMTP). Don't mock within the domain.\n146\t- Test the public boundary, not the implementation. Refactors shouldn't break tests; behavior changes should.\n147\t- Every bug gets a regression test in the same commit.\n148\t\n149\t---\n150\t\n151\t## Observability\n152\t\n153\tIf it can't be debugged from logs and the audit trail without reproduction, it isn't finished.\n154\t\n155\t- Every user-visible action emits a log line and, where appropriate, an audit record.\n156\t- Every error log includes request/user/tenant context.\n157\t- Every mutation crosses through the audit helper before `db.commit()` so the audit shares the business transaction.\n158\t- Webhook / email / external-service failures log at `warning` or `error` — never silently retry into the void.\n159\t\n160\t---\n161\t\n162\t## Done means done\n163\t\n164\tBefore claiming a task complete, confirm every checkbox:\n165\t\n166\t- [ ] Code change achieves the stated outcome — not \"mostly,\" actually.\n167\t- [ ] Build is clean (`cd frontend && npm run build` / `pytest tests -k <scope>`) with no new warnings you introduced.\n168\t- [ ] Diff contains zero `TODO` / `FIXME` / `eslint-disable` / `@ts-ignore` / silent `console.error` / bare `except:`.\n169\t- [ ] All N duplicates migrated, not just the first one.\n170\t- [ ] Docs updated in the same commit — manual, functional, architecture, todo, plan doc.\n171\t- [ ] Plan file moved to `docs/plans/archive/` if the plan is fully complete.\n172\t- [ ] Observability in place — new failure modes are diagnosable from logs + audit.\n173\t- [ ] Rollback plan exists for destructive or multi-step migrations.\n174\t\n175\t\"It works on my machine\" is a yellow flag, not a green light.\n176\t\n177\t---\n178\t\n179\t## When to decide vs when to ask\n180\t\n181\tReversibility + blast radius determine the answer.\n182\t\n183\t| Action | Default |\n184\t|---|---|\n185\t| Local reversible (edit a file, add a test, run tests) | Decide and proceed |\n186\t| Shared / visible (push, comment on PR, send message) | Confirm first unless pre-authorized for this exact action |\n187\t| Destructive (force-push, drop table, `rm -rf`, `--no-verify`) | Always confirm, even if authorized for a similar action |\n188\t| Infra-shared (deploy, restart prod service, change CI) | Always confirm, even in dev, unless the user named the specific action |\n189\t\n190\tMatch the scope of your action to what was asked. Authorization for one thing is not authorization for the next.\n191\t\n192\t---\n193\t\n194\t## What this isn't\n195\t\n196\tThis is the shape and quality layer. For project-specific framework gotchas (Enum columns, `text()` wrapping, bcrypt 72-byte limit, Pydantic v2 config, cookie + JWT auth, etc.) see each project's own `CLAUDE.md`.\n197\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016juPk15H1M5x3FpzubRVsP","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"84582203-6241-4514-ae71-fd306de07feb","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:31:13.317Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 0+1 task briefs"}
