{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_017VT4o5QN8AeT8TaaL74jGH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RXQRPcpMjzRHXZhmtpgtfK","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md","offset":1168,"limit":100},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4121,"cache_read_input_tokens":67437,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4121,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":41,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"cba58105-99cb-4df7-b29f-03de83b51ca9","request_id":"req_011Cb7MpWdNS5CKe5zCZjUgJ","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RXQRPcpMjzRHXZhmtpgtfK","type":"tool_result","content":"1168\t## 14. Internationalization & Localization\n1169\t\n1170\tEven though the platform ships in English today, every surface is built so that translation never requires a refactor. The cost of doing this from day one is small; the cost of retrofitting is enormous.\n1171\t\n1172\t- **Never concatenate strings to form user-facing text.** \"Hello, \" + name does not localize. Use template strings like `t('greeting', { name })` so translators can re-order tokens.\n1173\t- **All user-facing strings live in a single i18n catalog** (no inline literals in JSX — wrap with `t('…')` or your i18n hook). Even before we add a second locale, this gives us one place to edit copy.\n1174\t- **Numbers, dates, currency: format via `Intl`** (`Intl.NumberFormat`, `Intl.DateTimeFormat`). Never hand-format with `toFixed(2)` for currency or `padStart(2, '0')` for dates. Don't hardcode a `'en-US'` locale on `toLocale*` calls — pass `undefined` so the user's browser locale wins.\n1175\t- **Plurals use ICU MessageFormat** (`{count, plural, one {# user} other {# users}}`), not `count === 1 ? 'user' : 'users'`. The latter breaks for languages with non-binary plural rules.\n1176\t- **Mid-string interpolation uses ICU placeholders, not concatenation.** Prefer `\"priority_value\": \"Priority {value}\"` + `t('common.priority_value', { value })` over `<>Priority {value}</>`. The latter forces translators into English word order.\n1177\t- **Don't bake text into images.** SVGs that need text render the text as `<text>` so translation works.\n1178\t- **Bidi-friendly layout.** Use logical CSS properties (`margin-inline-start`, `padding-inline-end`) over `margin-left` / `padding-right` so RTL locales flip correctly.\n1179\t- **Allow text growth.** German is ~30% longer than English; allow 50% growth in button labels and table headers without truncation or layout collapse.\n1180\t\n1181\t### 14.1 Canonical setup (Cadres Portal)\n1182\t\n1183\t- `src/i18n/index.ts` initializes `i18next` with `LanguageDetector`, `i18next-icu`, and `initReactI18next`. The detector stores the chosen locale in `localStorage` under `cadres_locale`.\n1184\t- The catalog is a single JSON file per locale under `src/i18n/locales/<lang>.json`, keyed by feature namespace (matching the sidebar: `users`, `groups`, `tenants`, `applications`, `federation`, `auth`, `common`, …). New strings go into the namespace of the file that owns them; reusable copy goes into `common`.\n1185\t- Components import the bound `t` from `src/i18n` for module-level use (`import { t } from '../i18n'`). Components that must re-render on language change use `useTranslation()` from `react-i18next` instead — mostly irrelevant today since we ship only English.\n1186\t- Tests import `../i18n` in `src/test/setup.ts` so `t(…)` resolves against the real catalog instead of returning raw keys. Queries in Testing Library stay on the English strings.\n1187\t- When the tooling catches an untranslated literal (the `check-standards.sh` gate or review feedback), fix it in the same PR as the change that introduced it. Don't defer.\n1188\t\n1189\t---\n1190\t\n1191\t## 15. Theming & Dark Mode\n1192\t\n1193\tDaisyUI's theme system is the single source of truth for colors. Custom CSS that hard-codes `#fff` or `text-gray-700` defeats theming and breaks dark mode.\n1194\t\n1195\t- **Use semantic color tokens, not literal colors.** `bg-base-100`, `text-base-content`, `text-primary`, `border-base-300` — these adapt to the active theme. `bg-white` does not.\n1196\t- **Test every page in both light and dark themes** before merging. The CI gate doesn't catch this; design review must.\n1197\t- **Custom illustrations / SVGs** use `currentColor` for strokes and fills wherever possible so they pick up the theme automatically.\n1198\t- **Status colors** (success/warning/error/info) come from DaisyUI's semantic palette, which is contrast-tuned for each theme. Don't override them per-component.\n1199\t- **Theme selection** is per-user, persisted in localStorage. Honor `prefers-color-scheme` for the default on first visit.\n1200\t\n1201\t---\n1202\t\n1203\t## 16. Telemetry-Friendly Markup\n1204\t\n1205\tThe frontend is the cheapest place to add the breadcrumbs that incident response needs later. Bake them in now.\n1206\t\n1207\t- **Every interactive element gets a stable `data-testid`** for both Playwright tests and product analytics. Format: `data-testid=\"<feature>-<action>\"` (e.g., `data-testid=\"users-create-button\"`). IDs never change shape between releases; renaming one is a breaking change.\n1208\t- **Every page logs a `route_view` event on mount** with the route path and the current tenant_id. This drives the page-level analytics dashboard and the per-route latency telemetry.\n1209\t- **Every form submit logs `form_submit` with form name + outcome (success/error)** before the toast fires. Failed submits include the parsed error text — the same string the user saw — so support can correlate user reports to telemetry without asking the user to screenshot.\n1210\t- **No PII in event payloads.** Email addresses, passwords, tokens, secret values, even raw resource names go through a redaction layer before they leave the browser. When in doubt, send the resource ID and let the backend join.\n1211\t\n1212\t---\n1213\t\n1214\t## 17. References\n1215\t\n1216\tThe principles in section 1 and elsewhere in this document draw on widely-cited sources. When extending or interpreting these rules, consult the originals:\n1217\t\n1218\t- **Laws of UX** — [lawsofux.com](https://lawsofux.com/) — covers Doherty Threshold, Hick's Law, Miller's Law, Fitts's Law, Jakob's Law, Tesler's Law, Postel's Law, and others cited above.\n1219\t- **Nielsen Norman Group's 10 Usability Heuristics** — [nngroup.com](https://www.nngroup.com/articles/ten-usability-heuristics/) — the canonical heuristics cited as \"Nielsen #N\" throughout.\n1220\t- **WCAG 2.2** — [w3.org/TR/WCAG22](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/) — the normative accessibility specification. Section 3 of this document is the Cadres-applicable subset; consult the source for items not covered here.\n1221\t- **WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices** — [w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/) — patterns for keyboard handling, focus management, and ARIA roles.\n1222\t- **Inclusive Components** — [inclusive-components.design](https://inclusive-components.design/) — practical implementations of accessible widgets.\n1223\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"8b5f03c8-638e-4ef8-970f-7e8e62c1aa10","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:34:13.376Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
