{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01P3TAF2MweiNQAgL3QRv9FZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EZMz1JfXjae5f2UHe5woK5","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T21.md","content":"# T21 — Phase 3 — Memory stream taxonomy + scope enforcement helpers (deny-by-default)\n\n**Phase:** 3\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T19\n**Spec anchor:** [§ViLoMem](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#vilomem)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules.\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task carves the memory graph into the typed streams the spec calls out (code topology, workflow episodes, failure patterns, semantic repo claims, architecture decisions, user/team preferences, docs and contract state) and provides the scope-enforcement helpers that make sure every query is deny-by-default: a query without an explicit scope returns nothing, and cross-scope leakage (branch-only memory appearing in another branch, user memory leaking across users) is impossible at the store API. Without this task, retrieval (Phase 4) cannot ask \"give me only architecture-decision memories for this branch\" and verification (Phase 7) cannot enforce the spec's scope-leakage control.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"ViLoMem's transferable idea is dual-stream memory and retrieval. For code agents, generalize that to multiple typed memory streams: code topology, workflow episodes, failure patterns, semantic repo claims, architecture decisions, user/team preferences, docs and contract state. Each stream should have its own ranking rules, freshness semantics, and consolidation policy.\"\n\n> \"scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization\"\n\n> \"Risk: user/org/branch memories leak into wrong workspaces. Control: scope-aware queries, enforced filters in store APIs, and negative tests.\"\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: T19 — `MemoryClass`, `MemoryScope`, `MemoryRecord`, schema with denormalized `scope_*` columns.\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/classes.rs` (T19) — `MemoryClass`, `MemoryScope`.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/schema.sql` (T19) — denormalized scope columns that scope helpers query against.\n  - Existing scope conventions in `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/storage/` for workspace and branch identifiers.\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Naming (intent-over-mechanism), §Error handling.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. In `memory_graph/streams.rs`, define `pub enum MemoryStream` with the seven spec streams: `CodeTopology`, `WorkflowEpisodes`, `FailurePatterns`, `SemanticRepoClaims`, `ArchitectureDecisions`, `UserTeamPreferences`, `DocsAndContractState`. Derive serde + canonical wire names.\n2. Define `pub fn classify_stream(class: MemoryClass, assertion_type: AssertionType) -> MemoryStream` as the deterministic mapping the rest of the system uses. Examples per spec semantics:\n   - `Observation` / `Constraint` / `Pattern` / `AntiPattern` → `SemanticRepoClaims` or `CodeTopology` depending on whether the linked targets are symbols/files (code topology) or higher-level claims.\n   - `WorkflowOutcome` / `Procedure` → `WorkflowEpisodes`.\n   - `FailurePattern` → `FailurePatterns`.\n   - `Decision` / `ArchitectureInvariant` → `ArchitectureDecisions`.\n   - `Preference` → `UserTeamPreferences`.\n   - `DocsContract` → `DocsAndContractState`.\n   - `OpenQuestion` → `WorkflowEpisodes` (open questions belong to the active workflow stream until resolved into another class).\n   - `CounterMemory` → the stream of the memory it counters; if that memory is unknown, default to `SemanticRepoClaims` and flag via tracing.\n   Document the table in the module doc and cite `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-memory-graph-schema.md`.\n3. Define per-stream policy records: `pub struct StreamPolicy { stream: MemoryStream, freshness_default: FreshnessPolicy, consolidation_window: Duration, default_scope: MemoryScope, ranking_profile: RankingProfileRef }`. `RankingProfileRef` is a string id that retrieval (Phase 4) will resolve; do not embed ranking logic here.\n4. Provide `pub fn default_policy(stream: MemoryStream) -> StreamPolicy` returning the policy table referenced in the architecture doc.\n5. In `memory_graph/scope.rs`, define `pub struct ScopeFilter` as a typed builder: `session(session_id)`, `branch(workspace_id, branch_ref)`, `repo(workspace_id)`, `user(user_id)`, `organization(org_id)`. Builder methods consume `self` and return `Self`.\n6. Define `pub fn ScopeFilter::to_sql_predicate(&self) -> ScopePredicate` returning a parameterized WHERE clause and bind vector keyed against the denormalized `scope_*` columns from T19. Never `format!` user-supplied scope ids into SQL.\n7. Define `pub fn ScopeFilter::enforce_subset(of: &ScopeFilter) -> Result<(), ScopeError>` so a Phase 4 retrieval request cannot widen scope beyond what the session is allowed to see (e.g. a branch-scoped request cannot read org-scoped memory unless explicitly opted in by policy).\n8. Implement deny-by-default in the store APIs by requiring `ScopeFilter` on every read path: `MemoryStore::query(..., scope: &ScopeFilter)` — calling without a scope is a compile error. For internal admin/migration paths add `MemoryStore::query_unscoped_admin(...)` with a function name that makes the contract explicit and document it as test-or-migration-only.\n9. Define `pub enum ScopeError { Underspecified, CrossScopeViolation { requested: MemoryScope, allowed: MemoryScope }, IdentityMismatch { field: &'static str } }`.\n10. In `memory_graph/scope_tests.rs`, write tests covering: a query without a scope fails to compile or returns `Underspecified` (depending on whether the API takes `&ScopeFilter` directly); branch A's memory never appears in branch B's results; user A's memory never appears in user B's results; org-scoped memory only appears when explicitly enabled by `ScopeFilter::with_organization`; `enforce_subset` rejects widening; `classify_stream` mapping is exhaustive over `MemoryClass` (use a `match` with no `_` arm so the compiler enforces this).\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/streams.rs` — `MemoryStream`, `classify_stream`, `StreamPolicy`, `default_policy`.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/scope.rs` — `ScopeFilter` builder, `to_sql_predicate`, `enforce_subset`, `ScopeError`.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/scope_tests.rs` — stream classification exhaustiveness, scope leakage negative tests, subset enforcement, admin-path tests.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/streams.rs` — streams module exists.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/scope.rs` — scope module exists.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib memory_graph::scope_tests` — scope and stream tests pass.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n- Schema parity: `ScopeFilter` predicates reference the exact `scope_*` columns from T19; no new columns invented here.\n- No silent error handling — `ScopeError` returned on every misuse; admin path is named explicitly, not flagged via comment.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, commented-out code.\n- For SQLite: parameterized predicates only; `format!` of SQL is banned.\n- Use `tracing::warn!` on `CounterMemory` falling back to the default stream so operators can investigate.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content.\n- [ ] Every verification command passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: all seven streams, the deterministic class-to-stream mapping, all five scopes with deny-by-default enforcement, negative scope-leakage tests — all in this task.\n- [ ] Architecture doc updated in the same task if behavior is publicly observable — extend `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-memory-graph-schema.md` (T19) with `## Streams` and `## Scope enforcement` sections; this task is the authoritative source for the class-to-stream mapping.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13769,"cache_read_input_tokens":72090,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":13769,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KTEqX34f6x4nqNqXUnhApL","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"89252c77-0fc3-42ab-a384-665da933debb","request_id":"req_011Cb7N8P3xPmMeFRx8aHMR6","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 2+3+Gate task briefs"}
