{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01P3TAF2MweiNQAgL3QRv9FZ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TxC3qMMV8XEPPkGdS9Eizn","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T22.md","content":"# T22 — Phase 3 — Migration importer from existing Lattice memory rows\n\n**Phase:** 3\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** T19, T20, T21\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 3: Memory Graph Storage](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-3-memory-graph-storage)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules.\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task migrates every row from the legacy `memories` table (the 80+ column table referenced in the context inventory) into the redesigned `memories`, `memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, and `memory_scores` tables. Migration is idempotent, restartable, audited, and verifiable: every source row produces exactly one destination memory record plus zero-or-more derived link/evidence/access rows, all with provenance back to the source row id. Without this task, Phase 3 ships a parallel schema that the daemon cannot use without losing every existing memory; with it, the substrate switches over cleanly and the Phase 0 fork-or-extend decision is honored end-to-end.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"migration/import path from existing Lattice memory rows\"\n\n> \"memory can represent atomic notes, claims, workflow outcomes, procedures, preferences, and counter-memory\"\n\n> \"every memory can explain its evidence and relationship state\"\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - T19 — redesigned `memories` table and `MemoryRecord` shape.\n  - T20 — `memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores` tables and APIs.\n  - T21 — `MemoryStream` classifier and `ScopeFilter` (used to assign streams + scopes to migrated rows).\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/store.rs` — current `memories` table reader (the source of the migration).\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` schema definitions — the 80+ column legacy table layout.\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md` — legacy schema reference.\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-storage-migration-policy.md` (T03) — the policy this migration must follow.\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Schema / model parity, §Error handling, §No broken windows; Lattice rule that operational behavior is documented in Markdown.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Inventory the legacy `memories` table columns by reading the existing `memory/store.rs`. For each column, decide its destination: a column on the new `memories` table, a row in `memory_links`, a row in `memory_evidence`, a row in `memory_accesses`, a row in `memory_scores`, or \"dropped with rationale recorded.\"\n2. Write the column-mapping table in `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-memory-migration-guide.md` under `## Column mapping`. For every legacy column: destination, transform (if any), default value when null, drop rationale (if dropped). This table is the authoritative source — code references this section by heading.\n3. In `memory_graph/migration.rs`, define `pub struct MemoryMigrator { source_conn: Arc<Mutex<rusqlite::Connection>>, dest_conn: Arc<Mutex<rusqlite::Connection>>, batch_size: usize, dry_run: bool }`.\n4. Implement `MemoryMigrator::plan(&self) -> Result<MigrationPlan, MigrationError>` returning row counts per source table, per destination table, plus any skipped rows with reasons. The plan is rendered into the report before any write happens; this is the operator's chance to abort.\n5. Implement `MemoryMigrator::run(&self, plan: &MigrationPlan) -> Result<MigrationReport, MigrationError>`:\n   1. Open a transaction on `dest_conn`.\n   2. Iterate legacy rows in batches of `batch_size`.\n   3. For each legacy row: construct the destination `MemoryRecord` (assigning `MemoryClass` and `AssertionType` per the column-mapping table; assigning `MemoryStream` via `streams::classify_stream`; resolving `MemoryScope` from legacy `freshness_policy` + workspace context).\n   4. Insert the new `memories` row.\n   5. Extract embedded link/evidence/access/score data from legacy JSON blobs and insert into `memory_links`, `memory_evidence`, `memory_accesses`, `memory_scores`.\n   6. Record provenance: every destination row carries a `created_by = MigrationFrom { source_row_id }` actor variant so the migration's outputs are distinguishable from runtime writes.\n   7. Commit the transaction at the end of each batch so a crash mid-migration can resume.\n   8. Emit a `MemoryConsolidated` event per migrated record (via the T13 writer) with payload `{ source_row_id, dest_memory_id }` so the event log records the migration.\n6. Implement idempotence: maintain a `migration_progress` table tracking `last_migrated_source_row_id`; on `run`, skip rows already migrated. A second `run` after a successful first should produce zero new inserts.\n7. Implement `dry_run` mode: when true, build every destination row in memory and validate it against the new schema constraints, but do not write. Output a report identical to a real run so operators can diff plan vs. execution.\n8. Define `MigrationError` typed enum: `SourceUnreadable`, `DestinationUnready`, `RowMappingFailed { source_row_id, reason }`, `ConstraintViolation { table, detail }`, `IdempotenceConflict { source_row_id }`, `EventEmitFailed(EventWriteError)`.\n9. Add a CLI entry point `lattice memory-migrate --dry-run` / `--apply` so operators can run the migration outside the daemon's automatic boot path; document it in the migration guide.\n10. In `memory_graph/migration_tests.rs`, write tests covering:\n    - empty source DB migrates to empty destination with no errors.\n    - a curated set of legacy rows (one per memory class, plus a row with links, a row with evidence, a row with accesses) migrates exactly once; row counts match the plan.\n    - a second run after a successful first run is a no-op.\n    - a crash simulated by panicking mid-batch leaves the destination in a consistent state (the partial-batch transaction rolls back); a follow-up run completes the migration.\n    - `dry_run` produces a report identical to the real run but writes nothing.\n    - rows that cannot be mapped (e.g. unknown legacy class string) are reported under `RowMappingFailed` and the rest of the migration proceeds; the migration does not halt on the first bad row.\n11. Write `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-memory-migration-guide.md` with sections: `## Overview`, `## Column mapping`, `## Idempotence`, `## Dry-run`, `## CLI usage`, `## Recovery from partial migration`, `## Rollback procedure` (point at the Phase 0 storage policy's rollback path). The doc is the authoritative reference; cite headings from code.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/migration.rs` — `MemoryMigrator`, `MigrationPlan`, `MigrationReport`, `MigrationError`, `migration_progress` table DDL, CLI entry point integration.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/migration_tests.rs` — empty-source, curated-row, idempotence, crash-recovery, dry-run, partial-failure tests.\n- `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-memory-migration-guide.md` — authoritative migration doc with the mandatory sections.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory_graph/migration.rs` — migration module exists.\n- `test -f docs/architecture/2026-05-16-memory-migration-guide.md` — migration architecture doc exists.\n- `cd daemon && cargo test -p lattice-core --lib memory_graph::migration_tests` — every migration test passes.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file (split the column-mapping helpers into a sub-module if the file approaches the limit).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10.\n- Schema parity: the column-mapping table is the single source of truth; the code reads the destination shape from `MemoryRecord` so drift fails to compile, not silently at runtime.\n- No silent error handling — unmapped rows produce typed `RowMappingFailed` and appear in the report; never `unwrap`.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, commented-out code.\n- For SQLite: `rusqlite::params!` everywhere; transactional batches with explicit `commit`; `PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON` during migration.\n- Use `tracing::info_span!(\"memory_migration\", batch = ?)`, `tracing::warn!` on `RowMappingFailed`, `tracing::error!` on `ConstraintViolation`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content.\n- [ ] Every verification command passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: plan, run, idempotence, crash recovery, dry-run, partial-failure handling, CLI entry, and architecture doc all in this task. Operators have a complete, documented path from legacy memory to the redesigned schema.\n- [ ] Architecture doc updated in the same task if behavior is publicly observable — `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-memory-migration-guide.md` is created in this task.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":13769,"cache_read_input_tokens":72090,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":13769,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01KTEqX34f6x4nqNqXUnhApL","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"eb8a82f4-b880-4e2e-a6e4-936354febe50","request_id":"req_011Cb7N8P3xPmMeFRx8aHMR6","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 2+3+Gate task briefs"}
