{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_01KQKRzkV3LSKBqwLtHGVMhM","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Nwuw5hW1k4BrPTo5eoz4th","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T66.md","content":"# T66 — Phase 9 — Benchmark task fixture repository + golden anchors (cross-language)\n\n**Phase:** 9\n**Type:** tests\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** R64, T65\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-9-metrics-and-evaluation)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task ships the cross-language benchmark task fixture repository plus the golden-anchor file that defines the expected retrieval results for every benchmark task. Spec §Phase 9 names \"benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors\" as a binding deliverable, and spec §Risks \"Overfitting To Current Repo\" demands the control \"fixture repos across languages, repo sizes, and documentation styles.\" Without cross-language fixtures, every Phase 9 metric measures only Lattice-shaped Rust + TypeScript code and the success-criterion targets in spec §Measurable Success Criteria (30% fewer discovery calls, 40% fewer irrelevant reads, 80% memory precision, 90% relevant-test recommendation) cannot be claimed to generalize.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 9 Deliverables: …benchmark tasks with golden expected anchors; regression dashboard or CLI report.\"\n\n> \"Risk: benchmarks pass only for Lattice-like Rust/TypeScript repos. Control: fixture repos across languages, repo sizes, and documentation styles.\" (spec §Risks \"Overfitting To Current Repo\")\n\n> \"Initial targets: 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks; 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads; 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks; zero trusted display of known contradicted memory; zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label; 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks.\" (spec §Measurable Success Criteria)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs:\n  - R64 — MCP contract gate (tool surface is stable so benchmark inputs reference canonical names).\n  - T65 — metrics module (each fixture references the canonical `MetricSignal` names so the benchmark output can feed the collector directly).\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/benches/baseline_workflows.rs` (from T04) — pattern for criterion benchmarks; the new benchmark must reuse the same harness conventions.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/parser/` — language coverage today: TypeScript, Python, Rust, Go, Java, Markdown. The fixtures must exercise the same parsers so the benchmark catches parser regressions.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/benchmark_tests.rs` and `query/benchmark_tests.rs` — existing benchmark style.\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard `## Hard limits`, `## Schema / model parity`, `## No broken windows`, `## Tests as documentation`.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §Phase 9, §Measurable Success Criteria, and §Risks \"Overfitting To Current Repo\" in full. Quote the \"fixture repos across languages…\" sentence verbatim into `benches/fixtures/README.md` so the cross-language requirement is preserved at the fixture source.\n2. Author `benches/fixtures/README.md` describing the fixture layout: one sub-directory per language (`rust-repo/`, `typescript-repo/`, `python-repo/`), each with `manifest.toml` declaring repo size class (`small | medium | large`), documentation style (`sparse | moderate | rich`), and the benchmark tasks the fixture supports. Cross-link to spec §Risks.\n3. Create `benches/fixtures/rust-repo/manifest.toml` listing: name, size class, documentation style, list of benchmark task IDs, expected event counts on indexing, and a `golden_anchors_path` pointing into `golden_anchors.json`. Add the actual fixture source under `benches/fixtures/rust-repo/src/` shaped to exercise call graphs, trait impls, type refs, doc backlinks, and at least one cross-module test target. Include `Cargo.toml` and at least one inline Markdown doc citing source symbols.\n4. Create `benches/fixtures/typescript-repo/manifest.toml` and the TS fixture (with `package.json`, `tsconfig.json`, at least one React or Node module exercising imports, classes, interfaces, and tests). Document the size class and documentation style.\n5. Create `benches/fixtures/python-repo/manifest.toml` and the Python fixture (with `pyproject.toml`, a small module with classes, functions, tests, and a docstring-rich style). Document the size class and documentation style.\n6. Author `benches/fixtures/golden_anchors.json` containing one entry per benchmark task. Each entry: `task_id`, `repo`, `intent`, `task_prompt`, `expected_anchors` (list of stable `{file, symbol, doc_section}` identities), `expected_tests`, `expected_memory_classes`, `expected_render_mode`, `success_criteria_thresholds` (cited from spec §Measurable Success Criteria — e.g. relevant_anchor_recall ≥ 0.8). The JSON schema is documented in `benches/fixtures/README.md` and stable.\n7. Implement `benches/cognitive_workspace_benchmark.rs` using `criterion` (matching T04 style). For each fixture repo, the benchmark indexes the workspace, runs the redesigned tools (`prepare_change`, `get_context_capsule`, `find_relevant_tests`, `impact_from_diff`, `diagnose_failure`) against each golden task, feeds the resulting events into the T65 `MetricsCollector`, and reports the resulting `MetricValue`s. Reuse the existing `baseline_workflows.rs` patterns; do not duplicate harness code.\n8. The benchmark must run in CI-friendly time. Use `--warm-up-time 1 --measurement-time 3` as the default invocation profile (already encoded in the verification commands). The benchmark produces a JSON report that T68 ingests.\n9. Provide a deterministic seed and fixture freeze policy in `benches/fixtures/README.md`: fixtures change only via a documented bump that updates `golden_anchors.json` in the same commit.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/benches/fixtures/README.md` — fixture layout, size classes, documentation styles, golden-anchor schema, freeze policy; cites spec §Risks \"Overfitting To Current Repo.\"\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/benches/fixtures/rust-repo/manifest.toml` — Rust fixture manifest with size class, documentation style, supported task IDs, expected event counts, golden-anchor path.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/benches/fixtures/typescript-repo/manifest.toml` — TypeScript fixture manifest with same metadata.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/benches/fixtures/python-repo/manifest.toml` — Python fixture manifest with same metadata.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/benches/fixtures/golden_anchors.json` — per-task golden anchors, expected tests, expected memory classes, expected render mode, success-criteria thresholds.\n- `daemon/crates/lattice-core/benches/cognitive_workspace_benchmark.rs` — criterion benchmark indexing each fixture repo, running the redesigned tools, feeding events into the T65 `MetricsCollector`, and emitting the JSON report consumed by T68.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/benches/fixtures/golden_anchors.json` — confirms the golden-anchor file exists at the harness path.\n- `test -f daemon/crates/lattice-core/benches/cognitive_workspace_benchmark.rs` — confirms the benchmark file exists at the harness path.\n- `cd daemon && cargo build --release --benches` — the benchmark + fixtures compile cleanly with the rest of the workspace.\n- `cd daemon && cargo bench --bench cognitive_workspace_benchmark -- --warm-up-time 1 --measurement-time 3` — the benchmark runs to completion against all three fixture repos, exercising the redesigned tools and emitting a metrics report.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file (split fixture source into multiple files when needed).\n- Function length ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, cyclomatic ≤10 — applies to the benchmark harness code.\n- Schema parity: `golden_anchors.json` schema documented in `README.md`; every per-task entry has the same field set; the benchmark code validates the JSON schema before running so a malformed fixture fails fast.\n- No silent error handling — fixture indexing or tool failures fail the benchmark with a typed error.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code; no unjustified `#[allow(...)]`.\n- Public API names follow intent-over-mechanism naming (`GoldenAnchor::expected_render_mode`, not `GoldenAnchor::mode`).\n- For SQLite: benchmark uses temp DBs under `.lattice/` per-run isolation; cleans up on completion; no shared mutable state across fixtures.\n- Observability: benchmark emits `tracing::info!` spans per fixture per task with `fixture`, `task_id`, `tool`, `latency_ms`, `metric_signal_count` fields.\n- Doc citation: cite spec headings `## Phase 9: Metrics And Evaluation`, `## Measurable Success Criteria`, `## Risks` (specifically \"Overfitting To Current Repo\") in `benches/fixtures/README.md`.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command in tasks.json passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] No deferred work: all three language fixtures ship with full source + manifest + at least one benchmark task; `golden_anchors.json` is populated for every declared task; the benchmark runs end-to-end.\n- [ ] If a backward-incompatible change was needed, the AGENTS.md \"Backward-compatibility and contract discipline\" section was followed; the existing `baseline_workflows.rs` benchmark continues to run.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4161,"cache_read_input_tokens":95653,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4161,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":50,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_012n7S8LWZzY1cvitTHPzudV","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"6d774fa8-102a-454b-896d-3eed6756dcd8","request_id":"req_011Cb7NR9xtEZvzyrcHm57TP","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 8+9+Gate task briefs"}
