{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_019ScAFhwj15KgrT4jB8Uvi8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HnSssuUhJHmp9nJUExrmPp","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/pete/cadres/lattice/docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/tasks/T86.md","content":"# T86 — Phase 11 — Operator runbook + recovery playbook\n\n**Phase:** 11\n**Type:** docs\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** T82, T85\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 11: Hardening](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-11-hardening), [§Storage Design](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#storage-design), [§Documentation Requirements](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#documentation-requirements)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task delivers the two operational documents that turn the architecture suite into something an on-call human can actually use under pressure: the daily-operations runbook and the recovery playbook. The runbook is the day-in-the-life: how to deploy, how to verify health, how to read the metrics, how to respond to common alerts. The recovery playbook is the worst-day-of-the-week document: what to do when the daemon crashes, when the event log is corrupted, when memory is contradictory at scale, when migration breaks. It includes the binding deploy sequence from `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy and the replay-from-snapshot procedure proved viable by T82 recovery tests.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 11 deliverables: ... documentation for setup, operations, memory semantics, and workflow behavior.\"\n\n> \"Event log compaction: ... at configurable intervals, write a full-state snapshot of graph and memory to a separate file, then truncate the event log to events after the snapshot timestamp. The snapshot format must be versioned and independently readable so the daemon can bootstrap from a snapshot without replaying the full history.\" (spec §Storage Design — drives the replay-from-snapshot procedure)\n\n> \"Required docs: ... operator guide; ...\" (spec §Documentation Requirements — runbook is the deep operator-action surface)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: T82 (recovery + corruption tests — provides the proven procedures), T85 (documentation suite — provides the cross-link anchors for memory/event/consolidation/retrieval/verification docs).\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy — the canonical deploy sequence quoted in this runbook.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/hardening/recovery_tests.rs` (from T82) — the test that exercises the replay-from-snapshot procedure this playbook documents.\n  - `docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-operator-guide.md` (from T85) — the runbook cross-references its sections.\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-event-log-design.md` (from T85) — the playbook cross-references the compaction-snapshot semantics.\n- Standards files to honor: `coding.md`; `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy and Markdown heading references.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read T85's operator guide and event-log design doc so the runbook layers on top without duplicating their content.\n2. Create `docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-runbook.md`. Required headings:\n   - `## Daily operations` — required by verification. Cover: starting/stopping the daemon, checking health via the review panel from T76 (`consolidationQueueView`, `indexingHealthView`, `workspaceGraphHealthView`), reading the metrics report from T68/T69 (Phase 9), reviewing accepted/rejected promotions weekly.\n   - `## Deploy sequence` — quote the canonical sequence from `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy verbatim: `pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/`. Document the `sleep 2` between `pkill` and `cp` per the canonical sequence. Document the verification steps: confirm the new binary is running via `pgrep -fa lattice`; confirm the extension picks up the new binary via the activity bar.\n   - `## Health checks` — list the green/yellow/red thresholds for each indicator in the indexing health and graph health views: parser failure rate, vector index freshness, FTS row count, event log ingestion rate, compaction snapshot age. Each threshold cites the spec heading that motivates it.\n   - `## Alerts and response` — for each alert (queue depth at bound, dropped consolidation jobs, broken references, stale-edge spike, hot-path P99 budget breach), document the on-call response: which review-panel view to open, which log target to inspect, what to escalate.\n   - `## Memory hygiene` — operator weekly tasks: review promotion queue, review contradiction queue, audit stale memory, audit usefulness metrics from T68/T69.\n   - `## Backups` — document the backup strategy for `.lattice/` (graph.db, memories.db, events.db, snapshots, vector index) and the restore procedure (cross-link to the recovery playbook).\n3. Create `docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-recovery-playbook.md`. Required headings:\n   - `## Recovery procedures` — required by verification. Cover the full recovery decision tree: daemon won't start, daemon starts but indexing fails, daemon indexing succeeds but queries return empty, queries return stale data, memory state is contradictory, snapshot file is missing or corrupted.\n   - `## Replay from snapshot` — required by verification. The binding procedure proved viable by T82 recovery tests. Steps: (1) stop the daemon (`pkill -f lattice`); (2) verify the snapshot file exists at `.lattice/snapshots/<latest>.snap`; (3) verify the snapshot file integrity (file size, format version header, checksum); (4) move the existing `graph.db` and `memories.db` to a quarantine location for forensics; (5) start the daemon with `--bootstrap-from-snapshot <path>`; (6) confirm the daemon emits a `SnapshotBootstrap` event; (7) confirm queries return the expected pre-snapshot state plus post-snapshot replay; (8) document the post-recovery audit checklist. Cross-link to the test `test_replay_from_snapshot_reconstructs_state` in `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/hardening/recovery_tests.rs`.\n   - `## Replay from event log only` — fallback procedure when no snapshot is usable. Steps and post-replay verification.\n   - `## Quarantine and forensics` — procedure for preserving corrupted state for diagnosis without blocking recovery.\n   - `## Corruption recovery` — per-class procedures matching the T82 corruption test scenarios: corrupted payload, mismatched hash, invalid kind, invalid stable reference, truncated file, snapshot version mismatch.\n   - `## Rollback` — how to roll back a bad migration using the inverse migrations from T03's migration policy doc and T84's migration tests.\n   - `## Communicating incidents` — what to log, who to notify, what to capture for the post-incident review.\n4. Cross-link both documents: the runbook's \"Alerts and response\" section links to the recovery playbook for procedures that require recovery; the recovery playbook's intro links back to the runbook for daily-operations context.\n5. Confirm both documents cite spec headings exactly (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Markdown Heading References) — `## Storage Design` for compaction snapshots, `## 3. Event Log` for replay semantics, `## Phase 11: Hardening` for the operational invariants.\n6. Confirm the quoted deploy sequence matches `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy character-for-character.\n\n## Files expected\n\n- `docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-runbook.md` — daily-operations runbook covering deploy, health, alerts, memory hygiene, and backups.\n- `docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-recovery-playbook.md` — worst-day playbook covering replay-from-snapshot, replay-from-event-log, corruption recovery, rollback, and incident communication.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `test -f docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-runbook.md` — confirms the runbook exists.\n- `test -f docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-recovery-playbook.md` — confirms the recovery playbook exists.\n- `grep -q '## Daily operations' docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-runbook.md` — confirms the daily-operations section exists under its required heading.\n- `grep -q '## Recovery procedures' docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-recovery-playbook.md` — confirms the recovery-procedures section exists under its required heading.\n- `grep -q '## Replay from snapshot' docs/operator-guide/2026-05-16-recovery-playbook.md` — confirms the replay-from-snapshot procedure exists under its required heading.\n\n## Coding-standard checks\n\n- File length ≤800 lines per file.\n- No `TODO`, `FIXME`, `XXX`, commented-out code, no \"coming soon\" placeholders.\n- Single source of truth: the deploy sequence is quoted from `lattice/CLAUDE.md` exactly once; the runbook references the source rather than fork its own copy.\n- Doc citation: every procedural step that depends on a spec invariant cites the spec heading by exact text; every procedure that depends on a test cites the test name and file (per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Markdown Heading References).\n- Operator-friendly: every procedure is numbered, each step is a single action, each action is verifiable.\n\n## Definition of done\n\n- [ ] Every file in \"Files expected\" exists with real content (no stubs).\n- [ ] Every verification command passes from repo root.\n- [ ] Coding-standard checks satisfied.\n- [ ] Runbook quotes the `pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/` sequence from `lattice/CLAUDE.md` exactly.\n- [ ] Recovery playbook's replay-from-snapshot procedure cross-links to the T82 test that proves it works.\n- [ ] Both documents cross-link to each other and to T85's operator guide and architecture suite without duplicating content.\n- [ ] No deferred work: every operational scenario covered; R87 review confirms; R88 definition-of-done gate confirms.\n- [ ] After build, deploy per `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy procedure.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":101320,"cache_read_input_tokens":10845,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":101320,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XjrTa6YAS1dmMW989ZPauZ","session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"14e898a8-1634-43ba-a93c-adb05f08cca3","request_id":"req_011Cb7NGicAyFMeCwEAwKwU2","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Write Phase 10+11+Final task briefs"}
