{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NaxT2HJfaUnYW9Aa3kpoWp","type":"tool_result","content":"# T01 — Phase 0 — Fork-or-extend decision doc with evidence\n\n**Phase:** 0\n**Type:** foundation\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** none\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 0: Fork Foundation](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-0-fork-foundation)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task produces the binding fork-or-extend decision that every subsequent task in this build assumes. The spec gates Phase 0 on a \"fork only if necessary\" rule with four named gate conditions; this document evaluates each gate condition against current Lattice code evidence, names a branch or repo, and records the decision. The decision must precede any structural change because `depends_on` chains assume the layout, crate boundaries, and migration policy that this document selects. Without an explicit, evidence-backed decision, later phases would be free to silently diverge.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true: the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild; the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data; the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage; two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases.\"\n\n> \"If none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\"\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: none (this is the first task).\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` (current memory schema and storage)\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/graph/` (current identity and edge representation)\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/storage/` (current SQLite usage and WAL mode)\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md`\n  - `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/context.md` §4 \"Fork-or-extend decision\"\n- Standards files to honor: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (no broken windows, single source of truth); `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/AGENTS.md` (backward compatibility discipline).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md` §Phase 0 in full to internalize the gate conditions verbatim.\n2. Inspect `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/storage.rs` and `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/schema.rs` (or equivalent) and enumerate every column on the `memories` table; record which Phase 3 fields are additive vs. breaking.\n3. Inspect graph primary keys and edge representation in `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/graph/`; determine whether the Phase 1 identity model invalidates existing indexes.\n4. Inspect current SQLite connection setup in `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/storage/` and confirm WAL/journal mode; determine whether a new `events.db` file can coexist without WAL incompatibility.\n5. Evaluate each of the four gate conditions against the evidence and record a `met`/`unmet` verdict per condition with file/line citations.\n6. State the decision (`extend on long-lived branch` or `fork to new repo`) and name the branch or repo (e.g. `feat/cognitive-workspace`).\n7. Document the migration implication of the decision: which existing data must be preserved, which is rewritten, and the rollback path.\n8. List every downstream task that the decision binds (T02 architecture overview, T03 crate boundaries, T04 baselines, all Phase 1 backend tasks).\n9. Save the decision under `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-fork-or-extend-decision.md` with the mandatory headings checked by verification commands.\n\n## Files expected\n--- T19 ---\n# T19 — Phase 3 — Memory schema redesign (classes incl CounterMemory + required fields)\n\n**Phase:** 3\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** R18\n**Spec anchor:** [§Memory Graph](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#4-memory-graph)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules.\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task replaces the legacy single-table memory model with a typed memory schema that the rest of Phase 3 builds on. It defines the full memory-class taxonomy (including `CounterMemory` as a first-class class distinct from a `contradicts` link), the SQLite DDL covering every spec-required memory field, the Rust types that mirror the schema, and an architecture doc that records the model authoritatively so later phases (retrieval, consolidation, verification) reference one source of truth. Without this redesign, memory cannot represent claims, evidence, validity conditions, or counter-claims — only loose notes — which violates the spec's core thesis.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Primary memory classes: `Observation`, `Decision`, `Constraint`, `Pattern`, `AntiPattern`, `WorkflowOutcome`, `FailurePattern`, `Procedure`, `Preference`, `ArchitectureInvariant`, `DocsContract`, `OpenQuestion`, `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated.\"\n\n> \"Each memory record requires: stable id, content, memory class, assertion type, scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization, verification status, confidence and confidence reason, freshness policy, validity conditions, invalidation triggers, provenance events, evidence references, linked files, linked symbols, linked docs, linked tests, linked memories, contradiction links, supersession links, access history, usefulness scores, last verified state.\"\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R18 — the Phase 2 event log is approved; memory provenance can reference real `EventId`s.\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/store.rs` — current `memories` table (80+ columns) that T22 will migrate from.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/storage/` — SQLite connection conventions.\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md` — existing structured-memory doc that this task's new doc supersedes (cite it explicitly).\n  - Phase 1 identity types (`FileId`, `SymbolId`, `DocSectionId`, `TestId`, `MemoryId`, `EventId`).\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Schema / model parity, §Naming, §Single source of truth.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §Memory Graph in full and quote the 13 memory classes + the field list verbatim into doc comments on `MemoryClass` and the schema header.\n2. In `memory_graph/classes.rs`, define `pub enum MemoryClass` with all 13 variants: `Observation`, `Decision`, `Constraint`, `Pattern`, `AntiPattern`, `WorkflowOutcome`, `FailurePattern`, `Procedure`, `Preference`, `ArchitectureInvariant`, `DocsContract`, `OpenQuestion`, `CounterMemory`. Derive `Serialize`, `Deserialize`, `Clone`, `Copy`, `Debug`, `PartialEq`, `Eq`, `Hash`. Implement `as_str()` returning canonical snake_case wire names.\n3. Define `pub enum AssertionType { Observation, Decision, Constraint, Hypothesis, Procedure, Outcome, Preference, Question, Counter }` so the model separates \"what kind of structure the record has\" (class) from \"what truth-status it asserts\" (assertion type). Wire-canonical strings.\n4. Define `pub enum MemoryScope { Session, Branch, Repo, User, Organization }` matching the spec's five scopes. Deny-by-default at query time is enforced by T21; the enum is shared here.\n5. Define `pub enum VerificationStatus { Unverified, InReview, Verified, Stale, Contradicted, Superseded, Expired, Invalidated }` matching spec §Verification Engine outputs.\n6. Define `pub struct FreshnessPolicy { kind: FreshnessKind, ttl: Option<Duration>, recheck_interval: Option<Duration> }` with `FreshnessKind = { SessionScoped, BranchScoped, RepoScoped, TimeBound, EventTriggered }`.\n7. Define `pub struct ValidityCondition { description: String, predicate: ValidityPredicate }` where `ValidityPredicate` is an enum over machine-checkable conditions (file exists, symbol exists, doc heading exists, test passes, time before).\n8. Define `pub struct InvalidationTrigger { kind: TriggerKind, target: StableRef }` so verification (Phase 7) can subscribe deterministically: `TriggerKind = { FileChanged, SymbolChanged, DocSectionChanged, TestFailed, TimeExpired }`.\n9. Define the canonical `pub struct MemoryRecord` mirroring the schema below, with one field per spec-required attribute. Every non-private column has a corresponding field; nullability is matched precisely.\n10. In `memory_graph/schema.sql`, define the `memories` table (this is the new redesigned table, separate from the legacy one; T22 owns the migration). Columns, in order:\n    - `memory_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY` (stable id, UUIDv7 or ULID)\n    - `content TEXT NOT NULL`\n    - `class TEXT NOT NULL` (canonical `MemoryClass` wire name)\n    - `assertion_type TEXT NOT NULL`\n--- T71 ---\n# T71 — Phase 10 — Extension review-panel scaffolding + JSON-RPC plumbing to new tools\n\n**Phase:** 10\n**Type:** frontend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** R70\n**Spec anchor:** [§10. Human Review Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#10-human-review-surface), [§Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-10-human-review-and-extension-ux)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); UI specification (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task lays the foundation for the entire Phase 10 review surface. It registers a new VS Code webview view under the existing `viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice` slot in `extension/package.json`, wires a typed JSON-RPC bridge that translates the new memory/event/verification tool responses into UI state, and bootstraps the i18n catalog that every later subview (T72–T76) depends on. Without this scaffolding, the memory inbox, promotion queue, contradiction queue, stale view, event trace view, retrieval explanation view, consolidation queue, indexing health, and graph health subviews have no parent panel, no transport, and no string catalog. Spec §10 names the surface explicitly: \"This can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product.\" This task delivers that panel skeleton.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Required operator views: memory inbox; proposed promotions; proposed contradictions; stale memory list; memory evidence view; event trace view; retrieval explanation view; usefulness metrics; workspace graph health; indexing health; consolidation queue.\"\n\n> \"Definition of done: operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows.\"\n\n> \"Every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested.\" (spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties — binds the RPC bridge to the typed contract.)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R70 (Phase 9 metrics + evaluation review verdict) — confirms the metrics surface that the indexing/graph health views consume is stable, and that the new memory/event/verification tools introduced in Phases 6–9 have stable JSON shapes.\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `extension/package.json` — `viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice` is the activity-bar slot the new review view contributes to; the existing `lattice.sidebar` view confirms the pattern.\n  - `extension/src/extension.ts` — current activation function that the new review panel registration plugs into alongside the existing sidebar and docs workbench.\n  - `extension/src/daemon.ts` — current JSON-RPC client used to talk to the daemon over stdio; the new RPC bridge wraps this with typed request/response helpers for the review tools.\n  - `extension/src/sidebar.ts`, `extension/src/docsWorkbench.ts` — existing webview patterns to mirror (webview HTML build, message bus, dispose cleanup).\n- Standards files to honor: `coding.md` (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`, no inline error swallowing); `ui-specification.md` §3 (accessibility baseline), §7 (modals via native `<dialog>`), §8.1–8.5 (loading, empty, error states), §14 (i18n catalog); `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy procedure.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §10 Human Review Surface and §Phase 10 deliverables in full so the panel scope (eleven operator views) is explicit before writing any code.\n2. Open `extension/package.json` and add a new view contribution under `contributes.views.\"lattice\"` named `lattice.reviewPanel` with `type: \"webview\"`, an explicit title, and an icon path. Keep the existing `lattice.sidebar` view intact so the existing surface continues to function (per `AGENTS.md` backward-compatibility discipline).\n3. Add a command contribution `lattice.openReviewPanel` that focuses the new view, and bind it to the activity-bar group so the operator can launch the panel from the existing Lattice container.\n4. Create `extension/src/review/reviewPanel.ts` exporting a `ReviewPanelProvider` class implementing `vscode.WebviewViewProvider`. The provider owns the webview lifecycle (resolve, dispose), sets up CSP headers, and bootstraps the subview router that T72–T76 plug their views into. Set `enableScripts: true`, `localResourceRoots` restricted to the extension's `media/` and `out/` directories.\n5. Create `extension/src/review/rpcBridge.ts` exporting a typed `ReviewRpcBridge` class wrapping the existing `daemon.ts` client. Expose typed methods for the new tools introduced in Phases 6–9 (memory inbox listing, promotion queue, contradiction queue, stale memory, evidence inspection, event trace, retrieval explanation, consolidation queue, indexing health, graph health). Every method returns a `Promise<Result>` shape with explicit error variants — never swallow errors (`coding.md` §Error handling).\n6. Each `ReviewRpcBridge` method handler wraps its call in `try/catch` and surfaces failures via `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)` (the VS Code analogue of `parseApiError + toast.error` since `parseApiError` does not apply outside the Cadres web portal). Where a mutation result is also rendered into the webview, the bridge also posts a `{ type: 'error', message }` event to the webview so the subview can render an inline error state.\n7. Create `extension/src/review/i18n/en.json` with namespaced keys for every user-facing string the panel scaffolding emits today (panel title, \"loading\", \"no items\", \"error loading data\", retry label, \"unverified\"/\"verified\"/\"in_review\"/\"stale\"/\"contradicted\"/\"superseded\"/\"expired\"/\"invalidated\" status labels, accept/reject action labels). Provide a tiny `i18n.t(key, params?)` helper at `extension/src/review/i18n/index.ts` that reads from the catalog at module load. ICU placeholders only — never string concatenation (per `ui-specification.md` §14).\n8. Register `ReviewPanelProvider` from `extension/src/extension.ts` during activation, using `vscode.window.registerWebviewViewProvider('lattice.reviewPanel', provider)`. Wire the `lattice.openReviewPanel` command to `vscode.commands.executeCommand('lattice.reviewPanel.focus')`.\n9. Run `cd extension && npm install && npm run compile && npm run lint` and fix every error and warning before stopping. Confirm the new view shows up in the existing Lattice activity bar container without disturbing the existing sidebar.\n\n## Files expected\n\n--- R89 ---\n# R89 — Final readiness review — end-to-end fork acceptance\n\n**Phase:** Final readiness\n**Type:** review\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** R05, R10, R18, R25, R26, R33, R38, R47, R54, R55, R63, R64, R70, R78, R79, R87, R88\n**Spec anchor:** [§Implementation Plan](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#implementation-plan), [§Measurable Success Criteria](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#measurable-success-criteria), [§Risks](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#risks), [§Final Target](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#final-target)\n**Standards reviewed:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); UI specification (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md`); definition-of-done checklist (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md`); execution philosophy (`/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md`).\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis is the final gate before the cognitive-workspace fork is declared shippable. It assembles three matrices that each prior review touched only locally: (1) spec coverage matrix — one row per spec §Phase asserting PASS/PASS-WITH-CAVEATS/BLOCK; (2) measurable success criteria matrix — one row per target from spec §Measurable Success Criteria initial targets, with the evidence file path proving the target was hit; (3) risk controls matrix — one row per risk from spec §Risks asserting the control is implemented and pointing to the artifact that proves it. The review concludes with a single binding `## Ship verdict`: PASS or BLOCK. The verification step runs the full daemon test workspace and the full extension build to confirm the system compiles, lints, tests, and ships clean from a fresh checkout.\n\n## Inputs to read\n\n- Every prior review verdict: R05 (Phase 0), R10 (Phase 1), R18 (Phase 2), R25 (cross-layer contract gate identity+events+memory), R26 (Phase 3), R33 (Phase 4), R38 (Phase 5), R47 (Phase 6), R54 (Phase 7), R55 (cross-layer contract gate memory+verification+retrieval), R63 (Phase 8), R64 (MCP schema regression contract gate), R70 (Phase 9), R78 (Phase 10 frontend), R79 (extension↔daemon contract gate), R87 (Phase 11 hardening+docs), R88 (definition-of-done gate).\n- Every spec §Phase from §Phase 0 through §Phase 11.\n- Spec §Measurable Success Criteria — initial targets list.\n- Spec §Risks — every named risk.\n- Shared standards: `coding.md`, `ui-specification.md`, `definition-of-done-checklist.md`, `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` execution philosophy.\n\n## Review procedure\n\n1. Walk every spec §Phase (0 through 11) and assemble the spec coverage matrix. For each phase, locate the review file that covered it, capture the verdict, and propagate PASS / PASS-WITH-CAVEATS / BLOCK into this matrix. Any BLOCK from a prior review propagates to BLOCK here unless the blocking finding was resolved by a later follow-up task documented in this build.\n2. Walk every target from spec §Measurable Success Criteria → \"Initial targets\":\n   - 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks\n   - 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads\n   - 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks\n   - zero trusted display of known contradicted memory\n   - zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label\n   - 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks\n   For each, locate the evidence file (typically a benchmark report under `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/baselines/` or a test name under `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/hardening/` or `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/benchmark_tests.rs`). Record evidence file path + value observed + target met/missed.\n3. Walk every risk from spec §Risks (Opaque Memory Mutation, Ranking Complexity, Event Log Growth, Stale Memory Leakage, Scope Leakage, Overfitting To Current Repo) and confirm the control documented in the spec is implemented and tested. For each, cite the implementation file and the test that proves the control.\n4. Run the full verification suite from a clean state:\n   - `cd daemon && cargo build --release` — full release build.\n   - `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace` — full daemon test workspace.\n   - `cd extension && npm run compile` — extension TypeScript compile.\n   - `cd extension && npm run lint` — extension lint.\n   - `cd extension && npm test` — extension test suite including R79's contract tests.\n   Any failure blocks the ship verdict.\n5. Confirm the deploy sequence from `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy works end-to-end: `pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/`. Confirm the extension picks up the new binary.\n6. Confirm R88's `## Explicit Deferrals` section is either empty or every deferral meets the reason+owner+date bar; any silent deferral blocks the ship verdict per `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` execution philosophy.\n7. Confirm every spec §Non-Negotiable Product Property is satisfied (cross-reference with R83 workspace-boundary tests, R81 concurrency tests, R82 recovery tests, R87 hardening evidence).\n8. Confirm spec §Final Target is met: \"make a coding agent feel like it is operating in a workspace that remembers accurately.\" This is the qualitative final test — assemble evidence from R70 metrics + R78 frontend + R79 contract round-trips to argue the system delivers on each of the nine final-target bullet points.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"88204e0b-a61b-4ed7-ba98-4fcc53be0768","uuid":"321e8727-ab55-4627-a4fb-9397bf0eda2e","timestamp":"2026-05-17T02:50:00.426Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# T01 — Phase 0 — Fork-or-extend decision doc with evidence\n\n**Phase:** 0\n**Type:** foundation\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** none\n**Spec anchor:** [§Phase 0: Fork Foundation](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-0-fork-foundation)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task produces the binding fork-or-extend decision that every subsequent task in this build assumes. The spec gates Phase 0 on a \"fork only if necessary\" rule with four named gate conditions; this document evaluates each gate condition against current Lattice code evidence, names a branch or repo, and records the decision. The decision must precede any structural change because `depends_on` chains assume the layout, crate boundaries, and migration policy that this document selects. Without an explicit, evidence-backed decision, later phases would be free to silently diverge.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Phase 0 ends with an explicit fork/extend decision before any structural code changes begin. Fork if any of the following are true: the memory graph schema changes require breaking the existing `memories` table in a way that cannot be migrated without a full rebuild; the identity model requires changing the primary key or graph edge representation in a way that invalidates existing indexed data; the event log requires a new storage file or WAL mode incompatible with the current daemon's SQLite usage; two or more of the above apply and sharing a codebase would require sustained compatibility shims across all phases.\"\n\n> \"If none of those conditions hold, extend in place on a long-lived branch and skip the repo split. Document the decision and the evidence for it before Phase 1 begins.\"\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: none (this is the first task).\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/` (current memory schema and storage)\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/graph/` (current identity and edge representation)\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/storage/` (current SQLite usage and WAL mode)\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md`\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-stable-follow-up-handles.md`\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-storage-and-search-backends.md`\n  - `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/context.md` §4 \"Fork-or-extend decision\"\n- Standards files to honor: `/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md` (no broken windows, single source of truth); `/home/pete/cadres/lattice/AGENTS.md` (backward compatibility discipline).\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md` §Phase 0 in full to internalize the gate conditions verbatim.\n2. Inspect `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/storage.rs` and `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/schema.rs` (or equivalent) and enumerate every column on the `memories` table; record which Phase 3 fields are additive vs. breaking.\n3. Inspect graph primary keys and edge representation in `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/graph/`; determine whether the Phase 1 identity model invalidates existing indexes.\n4. Inspect current SQLite connection setup in `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/storage/` and confirm WAL/journal mode; determine whether a new `events.db` file can coexist without WAL incompatibility.\n5. Evaluate each of the four gate conditions against the evidence and record a `met`/`unmet` verdict per condition with file/line citations.\n6. State the decision (`extend on long-lived branch` or `fork to new repo`) and name the branch or repo (e.g. `feat/cognitive-workspace`).\n7. Document the migration implication of the decision: which existing data must be preserved, which is rewritten, and the rollback path.\n8. List every downstream task that the decision binds (T02 architecture overview, T03 crate boundaries, T04 baselines, all Phase 1 backend tasks).\n9. Save the decision under `docs/architecture/2026-05-16-fork-or-extend-decision.md` with the mandatory headings checked by verification commands.\n\n## Files expected\n--- T19 ---\n# T19 — Phase 3 — Memory schema redesign (classes incl CounterMemory + required fields)\n\n**Phase:** 3\n**Type:** backend\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** R18\n**Spec anchor:** [§Memory Graph](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#4-memory-graph)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard; Lattice project rules.\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task replaces the legacy single-table memory model with a typed memory schema that the rest of Phase 3 builds on. It defines the full memory-class taxonomy (including `CounterMemory` as a first-class class distinct from a `contradicts` link), the SQLite DDL covering every spec-required memory field, the Rust types that mirror the schema, and an architecture doc that records the model authoritatively so later phases (retrieval, consolidation, verification) reference one source of truth. Without this redesign, memory cannot represent claims, evidence, validity conditions, or counter-claims — only loose notes — which violates the spec's core thesis.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Primary memory classes: `Observation`, `Decision`, `Constraint`, `Pattern`, `AntiPattern`, `WorkflowOutcome`, `FailurePattern`, `Procedure`, `Preference`, `ArchitectureInvariant`, `DocsContract`, `OpenQuestion`, `CounterMemory` — a memory record that explicitly asserts a prior memory is wrong or no longer applicable. Distinct from a `contradicts` link: a link connects two existing memories; a CounterMemory is a first-class record authored by an agent or user that carries its own evidence, scope, and verification status and can itself be superseded or invalidated.\"\n\n> \"Each memory record requires: stable id, content, memory class, assertion type, scope: session, branch, repo, user, organization, verification status, confidence and confidence reason, freshness policy, validity conditions, invalidation triggers, provenance events, evidence references, linked files, linked symbols, linked docs, linked tests, linked memories, contradiction links, supersession links, access history, usefulness scores, last verified state.\"\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R18 — the Phase 2 event log is approved; memory provenance can reference real `EventId`s.\n- Existing repo files this task builds on:\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/memory/store.rs` — current `memories` table (80+ columns) that T22 will migrate from.\n  - `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/storage/` — SQLite connection conventions.\n  - `docs/architecture/2026-04-11-structured-memory.md` — existing structured-memory doc that this task's new doc supersedes (cite it explicitly).\n  - Phase 1 identity types (`FileId`, `SymbolId`, `DocSectionId`, `TestId`, `MemoryId`, `EventId`).\n- Standards: Cadres coding standard §Schema / model parity, §Naming, §Single source of truth.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §Memory Graph in full and quote the 13 memory classes + the field list verbatim into doc comments on `MemoryClass` and the schema header.\n2. In `memory_graph/classes.rs`, define `pub enum MemoryClass` with all 13 variants: `Observation`, `Decision`, `Constraint`, `Pattern`, `AntiPattern`, `WorkflowOutcome`, `FailurePattern`, `Procedure`, `Preference`, `ArchitectureInvariant`, `DocsContract`, `OpenQuestion`, `CounterMemory`. Derive `Serialize`, `Deserialize`, `Clone`, `Copy`, `Debug`, `PartialEq`, `Eq`, `Hash`. Implement `as_str()` returning canonical snake_case wire names.\n3. Define `pub enum AssertionType { Observation, Decision, Constraint, Hypothesis, Procedure, Outcome, Preference, Question, Counter }` so the model separates \"what kind of structure the record has\" (class) from \"what truth-status it asserts\" (assertion type). Wire-canonical strings.\n4. Define `pub enum MemoryScope { Session, Branch, Repo, User, Organization }` matching the spec's five scopes. Deny-by-default at query time is enforced by T21; the enum is shared here.\n5. Define `pub enum VerificationStatus { Unverified, InReview, Verified, Stale, Contradicted, Superseded, Expired, Invalidated }` matching spec §Verification Engine outputs.\n6. Define `pub struct FreshnessPolicy { kind: FreshnessKind, ttl: Option<Duration>, recheck_interval: Option<Duration> }` with `FreshnessKind = { SessionScoped, BranchScoped, RepoScoped, TimeBound, EventTriggered }`.\n7. Define `pub struct ValidityCondition { description: String, predicate: ValidityPredicate }` where `ValidityPredicate` is an enum over machine-checkable conditions (file exists, symbol exists, doc heading exists, test passes, time before).\n8. Define `pub struct InvalidationTrigger { kind: TriggerKind, target: StableRef }` so verification (Phase 7) can subscribe deterministically: `TriggerKind = { FileChanged, SymbolChanged, DocSectionChanged, TestFailed, TimeExpired }`.\n9. Define the canonical `pub struct MemoryRecord` mirroring the schema below, with one field per spec-required attribute. Every non-private column has a corresponding field; nullability is matched precisely.\n10. In `memory_graph/schema.sql`, define the `memories` table (this is the new redesigned table, separate from the legacy one; T22 owns the migration). Columns, in order:\n    - `memory_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY` (stable id, UUIDv7 or ULID)\n    - `content TEXT NOT NULL`\n    - `class TEXT NOT NULL` (canonical `MemoryClass` wire name)\n    - `assertion_type TEXT NOT NULL`\n--- T71 ---\n# T71 — Phase 10 — Extension review-panel scaffolding + JSON-RPC plumbing to new tools\n\n**Phase:** 10\n**Type:** frontend\n**Model class:** balanced\n**Depends on:** R70\n**Spec anchor:** [§10. Human Review Surface](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#10-human-review-surface), [§Phase 10: Human Review And Extension UX](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#phase-10-human-review-and-extension-ux)\n**Standards:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); UI specification (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md`); Lattice project rules (`/home/pete/cadres/lattice/CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`).\n\n## Goal\n\nThis task lays the foundation for the entire Phase 10 review surface. It registers a new VS Code webview view under the existing `viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice` slot in `extension/package.json`, wires a typed JSON-RPC bridge that translates the new memory/event/verification tool responses into UI state, and bootstraps the i18n catalog that every later subview (T72–T76) depends on. Without this scaffolding, the memory inbox, promotion queue, contradiction queue, stale view, event trace view, retrieval explanation view, consolidation queue, indexing health, and graph health subviews have no parent panel, no transport, and no string catalog. Spec §10 names the surface explicitly: \"This can be a VS Code extension panel or local web UI. It should be a review and trust surface, not the center of the product.\" This task delivers that panel skeleton.\n\n## Spec excerpts that drive this task\n\n> \"Required operator views: memory inbox; proposed promotions; proposed contradictions; stale memory list; memory evidence view; event trace view; retrieval explanation view; usefulness metrics; workspace graph health; indexing health; consolidation queue.\"\n\n> \"Definition of done: operators can understand and correct durable memory without reading raw SQLite rows.\"\n\n> \"Every public MCP contract is documented and regression-tested.\" (spec §Non-Negotiable Product Properties — binds the RPC bridge to the typed contract.)\n\n## Inputs\n\n- Dependency outputs: R70 (Phase 9 metrics + evaluation review verdict) — confirms the metrics surface that the indexing/graph health views consume is stable, and that the new memory/event/verification tools introduced in Phases 6–9 have stable JSON shapes.\n- Existing files in the repo this task builds on:\n  - `extension/package.json` — `viewsContainers.activitybar.lattice` is the activity-bar slot the new review view contributes to; the existing `lattice.sidebar` view confirms the pattern.\n  - `extension/src/extension.ts` — current activation function that the new review panel registration plugs into alongside the existing sidebar and docs workbench.\n  - `extension/src/daemon.ts` — current JSON-RPC client used to talk to the daemon over stdio; the new RPC bridge wraps this with typed request/response helpers for the review tools.\n  - `extension/src/sidebar.ts`, `extension/src/docsWorkbench.ts` — existing webview patterns to mirror (webview HTML build, message bus, dispose cleanup).\n- Standards files to honor: `coding.md` (file ≤800 lines, function ≤50 lines, nesting ≤3, no `window.confirm/prompt/alert`, no inline error swallowing); `ui-specification.md` §3 (accessibility baseline), §7 (modals via native `<dialog>`), §8.1–8.5 (loading, empty, error states), §14 (i18n catalog); `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy procedure.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read spec §10 Human Review Surface and §Phase 10 deliverables in full so the panel scope (eleven operator views) is explicit before writing any code.\n2. Open `extension/package.json` and add a new view contribution under `contributes.views.\"lattice\"` named `lattice.reviewPanel` with `type: \"webview\"`, an explicit title, and an icon path. Keep the existing `lattice.sidebar` view intact so the existing surface continues to function (per `AGENTS.md` backward-compatibility discipline).\n3. Add a command contribution `lattice.openReviewPanel` that focuses the new view, and bind it to the activity-bar group so the operator can launch the panel from the existing Lattice container.\n4. Create `extension/src/review/reviewPanel.ts` exporting a `ReviewPanelProvider` class implementing `vscode.WebviewViewProvider`. The provider owns the webview lifecycle (resolve, dispose), sets up CSP headers, and bootstraps the subview router that T72–T76 plug their views into. Set `enableScripts: true`, `localResourceRoots` restricted to the extension's `media/` and `out/` directories.\n5. Create `extension/src/review/rpcBridge.ts` exporting a typed `ReviewRpcBridge` class wrapping the existing `daemon.ts` client. Expose typed methods for the new tools introduced in Phases 6–9 (memory inbox listing, promotion queue, contradiction queue, stale memory, evidence inspection, event trace, retrieval explanation, consolidation queue, indexing health, graph health). Every method returns a `Promise<Result>` shape with explicit error variants — never swallow errors (`coding.md` §Error handling).\n6. Each `ReviewRpcBridge` method handler wraps its call in `try/catch` and surfaces failures via `vscode.window.showErrorMessage(err.message)` (the VS Code analogue of `parseApiError + toast.error` since `parseApiError` does not apply outside the Cadres web portal). Where a mutation result is also rendered into the webview, the bridge also posts a `{ type: 'error', message }` event to the webview so the subview can render an inline error state.\n7. Create `extension/src/review/i18n/en.json` with namespaced keys for every user-facing string the panel scaffolding emits today (panel title, \"loading\", \"no items\", \"error loading data\", retry label, \"unverified\"/\"verified\"/\"in_review\"/\"stale\"/\"contradicted\"/\"superseded\"/\"expired\"/\"invalidated\" status labels, accept/reject action labels). Provide a tiny `i18n.t(key, params?)` helper at `extension/src/review/i18n/index.ts` that reads from the catalog at module load. ICU placeholders only — never string concatenation (per `ui-specification.md` §14).\n8. Register `ReviewPanelProvider` from `extension/src/extension.ts` during activation, using `vscode.window.registerWebviewViewProvider('lattice.reviewPanel', provider)`. Wire the `lattice.openReviewPanel` command to `vscode.commands.executeCommand('lattice.reviewPanel.focus')`.\n9. Run `cd extension && npm install && npm run compile && npm run lint` and fix every error and warning before stopping. Confirm the new view shows up in the existing Lattice activity bar container without disturbing the existing sidebar.\n\n## Files expected\n\n--- R89 ---\n# R89 — Final readiness review — end-to-end fork acceptance\n\n**Phase:** Final readiness\n**Type:** review\n**Model class:** advanced\n**Depends on:** R05, R10, R18, R25, R26, R33, R38, R47, R54, R55, R63, R64, R70, R78, R79, R87, R88\n**Spec anchor:** [§Implementation Plan](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#implementation-plan), [§Measurable Success Criteria](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#measurable-success-criteria), [§Risks](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#risks), [§Final Target](../../2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-plan.md#final-target)\n**Standards reviewed:** Cadres coding standard (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/coding.md`); UI specification (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/ui-specification.md`); definition-of-done checklist (`/home/pete/cadres/shared/templates/definition-of-done-checklist.md`); execution philosophy (`/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md`).\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis is the final gate before the cognitive-workspace fork is declared shippable. It assembles three matrices that each prior review touched only locally: (1) spec coverage matrix — one row per spec §Phase asserting PASS/PASS-WITH-CAVEATS/BLOCK; (2) measurable success criteria matrix — one row per target from spec §Measurable Success Criteria initial targets, with the evidence file path proving the target was hit; (3) risk controls matrix — one row per risk from spec §Risks asserting the control is implemented and pointing to the artifact that proves it. The review concludes with a single binding `## Ship verdict`: PASS or BLOCK. The verification step runs the full daemon test workspace and the full extension build to confirm the system compiles, lints, tests, and ships clean from a fresh checkout.\n\n## Inputs to read\n\n- Every prior review verdict: R05 (Phase 0), R10 (Phase 1), R18 (Phase 2), R25 (cross-layer contract gate identity+events+memory), R26 (Phase 3), R33 (Phase 4), R38 (Phase 5), R47 (Phase 6), R54 (Phase 7), R55 (cross-layer contract gate memory+verification+retrieval), R63 (Phase 8), R64 (MCP schema regression contract gate), R70 (Phase 9), R78 (Phase 10 frontend), R79 (extension↔daemon contract gate), R87 (Phase 11 hardening+docs), R88 (definition-of-done gate).\n- Every spec §Phase from §Phase 0 through §Phase 11.\n- Spec §Measurable Success Criteria — initial targets list.\n- Spec §Risks — every named risk.\n- Shared standards: `coding.md`, `ui-specification.md`, `definition-of-done-checklist.md`, `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` execution philosophy.\n\n## Review procedure\n\n1. Walk every spec §Phase (0 through 11) and assemble the spec coverage matrix. For each phase, locate the review file that covered it, capture the verdict, and propagate PASS / PASS-WITH-CAVEATS / BLOCK into this matrix. Any BLOCK from a prior review propagates to BLOCK here unless the blocking finding was resolved by a later follow-up task documented in this build.\n2. Walk every target from spec §Measurable Success Criteria → \"Initial targets\":\n   - 30 percent fewer discovery tool calls on benchmark tasks\n   - 40 percent fewer irrelevant file reads\n   - 80 percent memory inclusion precision on curated memory benchmarks\n   - zero trusted display of known contradicted memory\n   - zero trusted display of known stale memory without stale label\n   - 90 percent correct relevant-test recommendation on curated tasks\n   For each, locate the evidence file (typically a benchmark report under `docs/plans/2026-05-16-cognitive-workspace-fork-build/baselines/` or a test name under `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/hardening/` or `daemon/crates/lattice-core/src/intelligence/benchmark_tests.rs`). Record evidence file path + value observed + target met/missed.\n3. Walk every risk from spec §Risks (Opaque Memory Mutation, Ranking Complexity, Event Log Growth, Stale Memory Leakage, Scope Leakage, Overfitting To Current Repo) and confirm the control documented in the spec is implemented and tested. For each, cite the implementation file and the test that proves the control.\n4. Run the full verification suite from a clean state:\n   - `cd daemon && cargo build --release` — full release build.\n   - `cd daemon && cargo test --workspace` — full daemon test workspace.\n   - `cd extension && npm run compile` — extension TypeScript compile.\n   - `cd extension && npm run lint` — extension lint.\n   - `cd extension && npm test` — extension test suite including R79's contract tests.\n   Any failure blocks the ship verdict.\n5. Confirm the deploy sequence from `lattice/CLAUDE.md` Build/Deploy works end-to-end: `pkill -f lattice && cp daemon/target/release/lattice extension/bin/ && cp daemon/target/release/lattice ~/.vscode/extensions/lattice.lattice-0.1.0/bin/`. Confirm the extension picks up the new binary.\n6. Confirm R88's `## Explicit Deferrals` section is either empty or every deferral meets the reason+owner+date bar; any silent deferral blocks the ship verdict per `/home/pete/.claude/CLAUDE.md` execution philosophy.\n7. Confirm every spec §Non-Negotiable Product Property is satisfied (cross-reference with R83 workspace-boundary tests, R81 concurrency tests, R82 recovery tests, R87 hardening evidence).\n8. Confirm spec §Final Target is met: \"make a coding agent feel like it is operating in a workspace that remembers accurately.\" This is the qualitative final test — assemble evidence from R70 metrics + R78 frontend + R79 contract round-trips to argue the system delivers on each of the nine final-target bullet points.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
